TP.register({id:"lp5-g7",lp:5,grade:"7",mode:"curriculum",checks:4,nextGrade:"8th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const FLOE_TITLE="Camp on a Moving Floor";const FLOE=["In winter the top of the Arctic Ocean freezes into a crust of sea ice. A wide slab of that floating ice is called a floe. Nothing holds a floe in place. Wind and ocean currents push it, so a research camp set up on a floe travels with it. One drift camp carried its crew more than 300 miles in five months, and nobody ever hitched a dog or started an engine.","A floor that moves can also break. For that reason every part of a drift camp is built to be picked up fast. Tents sit on wooden skis so a snow machine can tow them whole. Fuel comes in small drums one person can roll. Nothing is bolted down and nothing is buried. Two crew members walk a crack watch every six hours, tracing the edges of the floe with a drill and a tape. Each person keeps a packed bag by the tent door and knows the route to the thicker floe the team has chosen as a backup.","So why not work from solid ground? Because the questions the crew came to answer cannot be answered from anywhere else. Satellites photograph the top of the ice and measure how far it spreads, but they cannot tell how thick a floe is, and they cannot reach the water underneath it. Thickness takes a drill and a tape. The water below takes an instrument lowered on a cable through the drill hole, which reads the temperature and the saltiness at each depth. Those readings explain why some floes thin from below while the surface still looks solid.","So the crew spends its days measuring the one thing that makes the camp dangerous. A floe that cracks under a tent at two in the morning is also a floe that has just handed the team a new number. The moving floor is the laboratory."];const MEMO_TITLE="Switch the Inland Survey to Dogsleds";const MEMO_SRC="Memo from Dr. Rosalind Achebe, field leader, to the expedition board. October 2026.";const MEMO=["The inland survey should travel by dogsled this season instead of by snow machine. Three reasons follow, and then one more point from outside our team.","First, fuel. Each snow machine burns about 4 gallons a day on the survey route, and every gallon has to be flown in at $31 a gallon. Over a 40-day survey, two machines would need 320 gallons, which is $9,920 before a single repair. Dog food can be hauled on the sleds themselves, and last season our 16 dogs ate $2,100 worth of food across 40 days.","Second, safety on thin ice. A machine and its driver press nearly 700 pounds onto one narrow track. A dog team spreads about the same load across sixteen animals and a long sled, and dogs will stop and refuse to cross ice they do not trust. Our drivers have walked away from two machines that broke through in the past six seasons.","Third, the crew prefers them. All four people who drove a dog team on last year's coastal leg told me they would choose dogs again.","One more point. The Vostad team used dogs on the northern plateau last spring and reached the far ridge eleven days ahead of schedule, which shows that dogs are the faster choice."];const PHONE_TITLE="Buy the Second Phone";const PHONE_SRC="Memo from Dr. Idris Nakai, communications officer, to the expedition board. March 2026.";const PHONE=["Base camp should carry a second satellite phone this season. We now carry one. In the February storm its antenna iced over, and the radio log shows that camp was out of contact with the supply plane for 31 hours. During those hours a crew of four was out on the floe and could not be reached.","A second phone costs $2,400, which is less than one hour of flying time on the charter plane. Our own log lists nine days last season when weather or ice knocked the single phone offline for more than two hours. A survey at the university found that most students think polar research is interesting. With two phones, a camp can keep one warm inside a tent while the other rides on the sledge."];const STORY_TITLE="The Instrument and the Boot";const STORY=["Ines had been the station's radio operator for two winters, and she trusted numbers. The new thickness gauge stood on its tripod over the drill hole and gave a reading in four seconds: 162 centimeters, steady, with a small green light.","Pavel had been walking out onto the ice since before Ines was born. He did not look at the gauge at all. He walked, and every eight or ten steps he brought his heel down hard and listened.","“One sixty-two,” Ines said. “That is thicker than anything we crossed all week.”","“Mm,” said Pavel, and kept walking.","Ines wrote 162 in the log and underlined it, because the number was good news and she wanted the board to see it. Then she watched Pavel stop, go back two steps, and stamp in the same spot three times.","“Here,” he said. “Listen.”","She listened. The sound was not different, as far as she could tell. “The gauge reads the same thickness here.”","“The gauge says how far down the ice goes,” Pavel said. “It does not say what the ice is made of.” He crouched and scraped the snow away with his glove. Under it the ice was not white. It was gray and wet looking, full of small holes like a sponge. “Rotten,” he said. “Thick and rotten. A hundred and sixty-two centimeters of it.”","Ines looked at the number in her log and did not cross it out. She wrote a second line underneath: gray, porous, sounds dull underfoot. Pavel read it over her shoulder and said nothing, which from Pavel was a long speech.","On the walk back she asked him how long it had taken to learn the sound.","“Twenty years,” he said. “You will learn it faster. You have the gauge to argue with.”"];const HIST_TITLE="Judging the Ice Before the Gauges";const HIST_SRC="From The North Ice Stations, 1937 to 1991, a history by Margit Solheim. Chapter 4.";const HIST=["Before portable gauges, a drift station judged its ice by hand. Observers cut test holes with an auger and measured with a weighted tape. The method was slow, and it gave a number for one hole and nothing at all for the ground between holes.","So between holes the observers read the ice itself: the color of the surface where wind had swept the snow away, the sound a boot made on it, the way a sledge runner tracked across it. Station logs from those decades record both kinds of evidence side by side, often in the same entry. The written instructions issued to new observers in 1958 put it plainly: an auger reading is a fact about one hole, not about the route.","Electronic thickness gauges reached the stations in the 1980s. They were faster and they covered far more points. The logs show, though, that the old notes about color and sound did not disappear when the gauges arrived. They were written underneath the new numbers."];const BUOY_TITLE="Instruments Instead of Tents";const BUOY_SRC="Opinion column by Dr. Ngozi Farrell. Polar Instrument Review, 2026.";const BUOY=["A drift camp costs a season of four salaries, a chartered aircraft, and roughly nine tons of fuel and supplies flown onto moving ice. An autonomous buoy costs about the price of one charter flight. It freezes into the floe and reports thickness, water temperature, and saltiness every six hours for as long as three years, including the winter months when no camp is staffed.","Last year 214 buoys returned data from the Arctic basin. Between them they reported from more positions in one week than the staffed stations managed in a decade. Under-ice drones now run a line of readings 40 kilometers out from a single hole. None of this equipment needs a crack watch, and none of it has to be flown off a breaking floe.","The question is no longer whether people can live on the ice. It is whether the readings are worth the camp."];const SITE_A=[148,152,161,155,150,158,147,154,160,155];const SITE_B=[139,171,128,166,150,182,134,175,145,160];const siteTable=()=>F.table(["Site"].concat(SITE_A.map((v,i)=>String(i+1))).concat(["Mean"]),[["<strong>Site A</strong>"].concat(SITE_A.map(String)).concat(["<strong>154.0</strong>"]),["<strong>Site B</strong>"].concat(SITE_B.map(String)).concat(["<strong>155.0</strong>"])],{caption:"Ice thickness in centimeters at ten randomly chosen drill points at each site"});const para=(ps,st)=>ps.map(p=>`<p${st?` style="${st}"`:""}>${p}</p>`).join("");const pt=t=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea);font-size:1.5rem">${t}</h3>`;const block=(title,ps)=>`${pt(title)}<div class="passage sm">${para(ps)}</div>`;const floeBlock=()=>block(FLOE_TITLE,FLOE);const GAP="margin-bottom:.6em";const srcLine=t=>`<p class="src" style="font-size:.95rem;margin-bottom:.5em">${t}</p>`;const art=(h,src,ps)=>`<article><h3>${h}</h3>${src?srcLine(src):""}${para(ps,GAP)}</article>`;const oneText=(h,src,ps)=>`<div class="texts" style="grid-template-columns:minmax(0,46em)">${art(h,src,ps)}</div>`;const memoBlock=()=>oneText(MEMO_TITLE,MEMO_SRC,MEMO);const pills=a=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">${a.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const bullets=a=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:12px;font-size:1.35rem">${a.map(x=>`<li style="margin-bottom:6px">${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const dl=a=>`<dl class="card-list" style="padding-left:0">${a.map(x=>`<dt style="font-weight:700">${x[0]}</dt><dd style="margin:0 0 8px 1em">${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big" style="margin:0 0 6px;font-size:1.5rem">${t}</p>`;const fr=(a,b)=>`<sup>${a}</sup>⁄<sub>${b}</sub>`;const factBox=(title,list)=>`<div style="border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;padding:10px 18px;margin:12px 0 0;background:var(--bg)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.3em;font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.45">${list.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const lookBackStory=()=>lookBack("Look back at the story",block(STORY_TITLE,STORY));const srcCard=(head,body)=>`<div style="flex:1 1 250px;border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:10px 14px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px;text-align:left;font-size:1.2rem">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.45">${body}</p></div>`;const T=(x,y,t,a,fs)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="${fs||19}" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${t}</text>`;const svg=(w,h,width,alt,inner)=>`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="${width}" role="img" aria-label="${alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">${inner}</svg>`;function grid(o){const m={l:o.yl?62:34,r:18,t:o.title?34:16,b:o.xl?52:28},sx=o.sx,sy=o.sy,W=(o.x1-o.x0)*sx,H=(o.y1-o.y0)*sy;const X=x=>m.l+(x-o.x0)*sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*sy,gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy,fs=o.fs||13;let s=`<svg ${o.cls?`class="${o.cls}" `:""}viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${o.w||W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;if(o.title)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="20" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=o.x0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=o.y0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${m.t+H}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${m.l}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${m.l}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let x=o.x0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx)s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${m.t+H+fs+4}" font-size="${fs}" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${x}</text>`;for(let y=o.y0+ly;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly)s+=`<text x="${m.l-6}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="${fs}" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${y}</text>`;if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+42}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(16,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="${p.c||"var(--sea)"}" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="${o.pr||5.5}" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2"/>`});return s+"</svg>"}const genA=()=>F.table(["Hours run","3","5","8"],[["Liters of fuel used","7.2","12.0","19.2"]],{caption:"Generator A"});const genB=()=>F.table(["Hours run","3","5","8"],[["Liters of fuel used","7.5","12.0","20.0"]],{caption:"Generator B"});const heatTable=()=>F.table(["Minutes the heater has run","0","10","20","30"],[["Tank pressure (psi)","60","52","44","36"]],{caption:"Tent heater tank"});const teamGraph=()=>grid({alt:"Graph titled Dogsled Team Bravo. Miles traveled against hours of travel. A straight line from the point 0 comma 0 through 2 comma 18, 4 comma 36, and 6 comma 54.",title:"Dogsled Team Bravo",x0:0,x1:6,y0:0,y1:54,sx:32,sy:3.2,gx:1,lx:1,gy:9,ly:9,xl:"Hours of travel",yl:"Miles traveled",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[6,54]]}],pts:[[2,18],[4,36],[6,54]]});const teamAGraph=()=>grid({alt:"Graph titled Team Alfa. Miles traveled against hours of travel. A straight line from the point 0 comma 0 through 3 comma 24 and 6 comma 48.",title:"Team Alfa",x0:0,x1:6,y0:0,y1:48,sx:32,sy:3.4,gx:1,lx:1,gy:8,ly:8,xl:"Hours of travel",yl:"Miles traveled",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[6,48]]}],pts:[[3,24],[6,48]]});function depthFig(){return svg(470,286,420,"Side view of the water under the camp. A line near the top is labeled sea surface equals 0 feet, with a camp box sitting on it. The water below is shaded. A line near the bottom is labeled sea floor equals negative 238 feet. A cable hangs straight down from the surface to the sea floor. A marker one fourth of the way down is labeled instrument, with a question mark.",`<rect x="0" y="62" width="470" height="180" fill="var(--sea-soft)"/><rect x="0" y="242" width="470" height="44" fill="var(--gold)" fill-opacity=".45"/>
     <rect x="318" y="30" width="120" height="30" rx="5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>${T(378,51,"camp",null,16)}
     <line x1="0" y1="62" x2="470" y2="62" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="0" y1="242" x2="470" y2="242" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/>
     <line x1="200" y1="62" x2="200" y2="242" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
     <circle cx="200" cy="107" r="9" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
     <line x1="213" y1="107" x2="248" y2="107" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
     ${T(254,102,"instrument: ¼ of the","start",15)}${T(254,122,"way down = ?","start",15)}
     ${T(8,54,"sea surface = 0 ft","start",16)}${T(8,234,"sea floor = −238 ft","start",16)}`)}function lidFig(){const cx=180,cy=140,r=112;return svg(360,290,340,"A circle with a line drawn straight across through its center, labeled 56 centimeters.",`<circle cx="${cx}" cy="${cy}" r="${r}" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/>
    <line x1="${cx-r}" y1="${cy}" x2="${cx+r}" y2="${cy}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
    <circle cx="${cx}" cy="${cy}" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"/>
    ${T(cx,cy-12,"56 cm")}${T(cx,cy+r+30,"fuel drum lid")}`)}function floorPlanFig(){const ox=60,oy=50,w=240,h=180,tip=84;return svg(470,300,450,"A floor plan: a rectangle 4 meters by 3 meters, with a triangle joined to its right end. The triangle has a base of 3 meters and a dashed height line of 1.4 meters.",`<rect x="${ox}" y="${oy}" width="${w}" height="${h}" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3"/>
    <polygon points="${ox+w},${oy} ${ox+w+tip},${oy+h/2} ${ox+w},${oy+h}" fill="var(--gold)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
    <line x1="${ox+w}" y1="${oy+h/2}" x2="${ox+w+tip}" y2="${oy+h/2}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="7 6"/>
    ${T(ox+w/2,oy+h+30,"4 m")}${T(ox-12,oy+h/2+6,"3 m","end")}${T(ox+w+tip/2+6,oy+h/2-18,"1.4 m")}
    ${T(ox+w/2,oy-16,"sleeping area")}${T(ox+w+tip/2+30,oy+h-10,"vestibule","start")}`)}const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up and read it to your teacher.";const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Camp Briefing",short:"Briefing",parts:[{std:["RI.7.2"],dur:140,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"two central ideas, how they develop, where the text turns, and an objective summary of an article",html:floeBlock,qs:["This article has two central ideas. What are they?","How does the author build each idea? Give one detail for each.","Where does the article turn from the first idea to the second? Which sentence marks the turn?","Give an objective summary of the article in 2 or 3 sentences. Tell what it says, not what you think of it."],key:["(1) A drift camp sits on sea ice that moves and can break, so the whole camp is built to be moved in a hurry. (2) The crew accepts that risk because ice thickness and the water underneath can only be measured from the ice itself, not from satellites. Needs both ideas. A topic alone, such as “sea ice,” is not enough. (RI.7.2, two central ideas)","Idea 1 details: wind and currents push the floe, and one camp drifted more than 300 miles in five months; tents sit on wooden skis; fuel comes in drums one person can roll; nothing is bolted down or buried; a crack watch every six hours; a packed bag by the tent door and a backup floe. Idea 2 details: satellites photograph the top of the ice and measure how far it spreads but cannot tell how thick a floe is; thickness takes a drill and a tape; an instrument on a cable reads temperature and saltiness at each depth; those readings explain why some floes thin from below. Needs one detail for each idea. (RI.7.2, development of the central ideas)","At the start of the third paragraph. The question “So why not work from solid ground?” marks the turn. Before it the article is about the moving, breaking ice and how the camp handles it. After it the article is about why the crew is out on the ice at all. (RI.7.2, analyzing how the ideas develop over the text)","Covers both ideas with no opinion, for example: A drift camp is built on a floating slab of sea ice that wind and current push along and that can crack at any time, so the tents, the fuel, and the daily routines are all set up for a fast move. The crew takes that risk because thickness and the temperature and saltiness of the water below can only be measured from the ice, while satellites see only the surface. Those readings show why some floes thin from underneath. Leaves out judgments such as “that sounds scary.” (RI.7.2, objective summary)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Fuel and Miles",short:"Fuel & Miles",parts:[{std:["7.RP.A.2"],dur:130,dir:"Look at the tables and the graph. Answer out loud. Use your paper if you need it.",desc:"recognizing and representing proportional relationships in tables, an equation, and a graph, and telling a constant rate apart from a proportional relationship",html:()=>lead("Base camp runs two generators, tracks a dogsled team, and watches the pressure in the tent heater tank."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="align-items:flex-start;gap:26px;flex-wrap:wrap">${genA()}${genB()}<div>${teamGraph()}</div>${heatTable()}</div>`,qs:["Which generator's table shows a proportional relationship between hours run and liters of fuel used? How do you know?","For that generator, what is the constant of proportionality and what does it mean at camp? Write an equation for the liters of fuel, f, used in h hours, then find the fuel for 14 hours.","Look at the graph for Dogsled Team Bravo. How can you tell from the graph that miles are proportional to hours? What does the point (4, 36) mean? What is the unit rate, and which point on the line shows it?","Look at the tent heater table. Is the tank pressure proportional to the minutes the heater has run? Explain. If it is not proportional, say what is true about the pressure instead."],key:["Generator A. Liters ÷ hours is the same every time: 7.2 ÷ 3 = 2.4, 12.0 ÷ 5 = 2.4, 19.2 ÷ 8 = 2.4. For Generator B the ratios change: 7.5 ÷ 3 = 2.5, 12.0 ÷ 5 = 2.4, 20.0 ÷ 8 = 2.5. Or: graph both tables and see which gives a straight line through the origin. Partial: the right generator named with only one ratio checked is approaching. (7.RP.A.2, test for a proportional relationship)","The constant of proportionality is 2.4, which means Generator A burns 2.4 liters of fuel each hour. Equation: f = 2.4h. In 14 hours it burns 33.6 liters, because 2.4 × 14 = 33.6. Or: f = h × 2.4 counts equally. Partial: two of the three parts answered is approaching. (7.RP.A.2, constant of proportionality and equation)","It is a straight line and it passes through the origin, (0, 0). The point (4, 36) means the team travels 36 miles in 4 hours. The unit rate is 9 miles each hour, because 36 ÷ 4 = 9, and the point (1, 9) shows it. Partial: three of the four parts answered is approaching. (7.RP.A.2, meaning of a point and unit rate on a graph)","No, it is not proportional. At 0 minutes the pressure is already 60 psi, so a graph of the table would not pass through (0, 0). The ratios also change: 52 ÷ 10 = 5.2 but 44 ÷ 20 = 2.2. What is true is that the pressure drops at a constant rate, 8 psi every 10 minutes. A constant rate of change is not the same thing as a proportional relationship. Partial: saying no with no reason from (0, 0) and no ratios is approaching. (7.RP.A.2, deciding whether a relationship is proportional)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Radio Log",short:"Radio Log",parts:[{std:["L.7.1"],dur:180,dir:"Answer out loud. Say each new or fixed sentence the correct way.",desc:"spoken grammar items (phrases and clauses, the four sentence types, combining to show contrast, misplaced and dangling modifiers)",qs:["“The radio operator logged the wind speed <u>before the storm reached camp</u>.” Is the underlined part a phrase or a clause? What job does it do in the sentence?","“A sled <u>loaded with fuel drums</u> takes two people to turn.” Is the underlined part a phrase or a clause? Which word does it describe?","Is this sentence simple, compound, complex, or compound-complex? “Although the sky was clear, the team stayed in the tent, and the cook melted snow for water.”","Is this sentence simple, compound, complex, or compound-complex? “The depth sounder beeped twice and then went quiet.”","Join these into ONE complex sentence that shows a contrast. Use although or while. “The ice was two feet thick near shore. It was only four inches thick past the point.”","Something sounds wrong here. Fix it: “We spotted a whale eating lunch on the deck.”","Something sounds wrong here. Fix it: “After drilling through the ice, the water temperature was measured.”"],key:["A clause. It has a subject (storm) and a verb (reached), but it cannot stand alone because of the word before. It tells when the operator logged the speed, so it works like an adverb. (L.7.1, function of a clause)","A phrase. There is no subject and verb pair inside it. It describes sled, so it works like an adjective. (L.7.1, function of a phrase)","Compound-complex. It has one dependent clause (Although the sky was clear) and two independent clauses (the team stayed in the tent, the cook melted snow for water) joined by and. (L.7.1, sentence types)","Simple. There is one subject (sounder) with two verbs, beeped and went. Two verbs do not make a compound sentence, because there is still only one independent clause. (L.7.1, sentence types)","Although the ice was two feet thick near shore, it was only four inches thick past the point. Also correct: While the ice was two feet thick near shore, it was only four inches thick past the point. Or: The ice was two feet thick near shore, although it was only four inches thick past the point. Needs a dependent clause that carries the contrast. (L.7.1, choosing a sentence type to show a relationship)","Eating lunch on the deck, we spotted a whale. Also correct: While we were eating lunch on the deck, we spotted a whale. As written, the whale is eating lunch on the deck. (L.7.1, misplaced modifier)","Names who did the drilling, such as: After drilling through the ice, the team measured the water temperature. Also correct: After the team drilled through the ice, it measured the water temperature. As written, nobody is doing the drilling, so the sentence reads as if the temperature drilled the hole. (L.7.1, dangling modifier)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Flag the Unknown",short:"Unknown",parts:[{std:["7.EE.B.4"],dur:150,dir:"For each one, write your own equation or inequality on your paper, solve it, and hold it up.",desc:"writing equations and an inequality from expedition situations, solving them, and saying what the solution means",qs:["A dogsled outfitter charges a $65 trip fee plus $18 for each day of the trip. The expedition paid $353 in all. Write an equation and solve it to find the number of days.","Four supply crates are packed the same way. Each crate holds some ration bars plus 6 fuel tablets. The four crates hold 104 items altogether. Write an equation and solve it to find the number of ration bars in one crate.","The radio battery has 240 minutes of talk time left, and the team must keep at least 60 minutes in reserve for an emergency. Each daily check-in call uses 15 minutes. Write an inequality for the number of check-in calls, c, the team can make. Solve it and tell what your answer means.","For 6 hours straight, the temperature at the weather station fell by the same number of degrees each hour. It started at 3 °C and ended at −15 °C. Write an equation that uses a variable for the hourly change, solve it, and say what the answer means."],key:["18d + 65 = 353. Subtract 65: 18d = 288. Divide by 18: d = 16 days. Or: 353 − 65 = 288, then 288 ÷ 18, with the equation written too. Partial: the equation right with no solution is approaching. (7.EE.B.4, writing and solving px + q = r)","4(b + 6) = 104. Divide by 4: b + 6 = 26. Subtract 6: b = 20 ration bars. Also correct: 4b + 24 = 104, so 4b = 80. (7.EE.B.4, writing and solving p(x + q) = r). Partial: 26 given as the answer leaves the fuel tablets in and is approaching.","240 − 15c ≥ 60, or 15c ≤ 180. Either way, c ≤ 12. The team can make at most 12 check-in calls, so 0 to 12 calls, and still keep 60 minutes in reserve. Partial: c ≤ 12 with nothing said about what it means is approaching. (7.EE.B.4, writing an inequality and explaining the solution)","6d + 3 = −15. Subtract 3: 6d = −18. Divide by 6: d = −3. The temperature fell 3 degrees each hour. A negative answer makes sense here because the temperature was going down. Or: the whole change is −18 over 6 hours, so −18 ÷ 6 = −3, with the equation written too. Partial: d = 3 with no sign is approaching. (7.EE.B.4, writing an equation with a negative solution and explaining it)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Dogs or Machines?",short:"Dogs or Machines",parts:[{std:["RI.7.8"],dur:130,dir:"Read the memo to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"tracing the argument in a memo and judging whether the reasoning is sound and the evidence is relevant and sufficient",html:memoBlock,qs:["What is Dr. Achebe's claim? What reasons does she give for it?","Which reason has the strongest evidence? Explain why that evidence is on topic and enough.","Two parts of her argument rest on weak evidence. Name one of them and tell what is wrong with the reasoning."],key:["Claim: the inland survey should travel by dogsled this season instead of by snow machine. Reasons: flown-in fuel for the machines is expensive; dogs are safer on thin ice; the crew prefers dogs. She then adds one more point from outside the team, that a rival team using dogs finished ahead of schedule. Needs the claim and at least two reasons. (RI.7.8, trace the argument)","The fuel reason. It gives measured amounts and prices for this route (about 4 gallons a day for each machine, $31 a gallon, 320 gallons and $9,920 over a 40-day survey) and sets them against a real figure from the team's own last season ($2,100 of dog food for 16 dogs across the same 40 days). The numbers cover the same route and the same number of days, so they are on topic, and they price both choices, so they are enough to compare. Also accept the thin-ice reason if the student notes that the weight comparison is on topic but two break-throughs in six seasons is thin. (RI.7.8, relevant and sufficient evidence)","Either one. The Vostad team point: finishing eleven days early does not show that dogs are faster, because that was a different crew on a different route in different weather, and the memo rules out no other reason for the early finish. Or the crew preference reason: the four people asked had already chosen to drive dogs, so they are not a fair sample, and liking a method is not evidence that it works better. Needs the part named and what is wrong with the reasoning. (RI.7.8, soundness of reasoning)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Below Zero",short:"Below Zero",parts:[{title:"Cold and Deep",std:["7.NS.A.3"],dur:120,dir:"Solve each one. Tell your teacher the answer with its sign and its units.",desc:"real-world expedition problems that use the four operations with negative decimals and fractions",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${depthFig()}</div>`,qs:["At noon the weather station read −12.4 °C. By 6 p.m. the temperature had risen 3.9 degrees. What did the station read at 6 p.m.?","Look at the picture. A depth sounding gives the sea floor under the camp as −238 feet, which means 238 feet below the surface. An instrument hangs on the cable one fourth of the way down from the surface to the sea floor. What is the instrument's depth?","Over 5 nights the change in snow depth at the tent door was +2.5 inches, −1.25 inches, +0.75 inch, −3 inches, and +1.5 inches. What was the total change? What was the mean change for one night?","A weather balloon carries a sensor upward. The air temperature falls 2.5 °C for every 1,000 feet the balloon rises. The sensor reads −8 °C at camp. What does it read after the balloon rises 4,000 feet?"],key:["−8.5 °C. −12.4 + 3.9 = −8.5. Or: count up 3.9 from −12.4 on a number line. (7.NS.A.3, adding rational numbers)",`−59.5 feet, which is 59.5 feet below the surface. ${fr(1,4)} × (−238) = −59.5, or −238 ÷ 4 = −59.5. Partial: 59.5 feet given with no sign and no direction is approaching. (7.NS.A.3, multiplying a fraction by a negative number)`,"Total change: +0.5 inch. 2.5 − 1.25 + 0.75 − 3 + 1.5 = 0.5. Mean: +0.1 inch a night, because 0.5 ÷ 5 = 0.1. Or: add the three rises to get 4.75 and the two falls to get −4.25. Partial: the total right with no mean is approaching. (7.NS.A.3, adding and dividing rational numbers)","−18 °C. The rise is 4 thousands of feet, so the change is 4 × (−2.5) = −10. Then −8 + (−10) = −18. Or: step down 2.5 four times from −8, giving −10.5, −13, −15.5, −18. (7.NS.A.3, multiplying and adding rational numbers)"]},{title:"Rope, Rations, Drums",std:["7.EE.B.3"],dur:130,dir:"Solve on your paper. Show your steps and your estimate, then hold it up.",desc:"multi-step problems with fractions, decimals, and percents, checked against an estimate",qs:[`Guy lines are the ropes that hold a tent down. A coil of rope is 48${fr(3,4)} feet long. The crew cuts 5 guy lines that are each 7.4 feet long. How much rope is left on the coil?`,`Each crew member needs 3${fr(1,2)} pounds of food a day. There are 6 members, and the sledge trip lasts 9 days. First estimate the total weight of food. Then find the exact weight. Does your exact answer make sense?`,"A 55-gallon fuel drum costs $300 at the port depot, before any flight. The supplier raises the price by 8%, and then the expedition takes a 25% research discount off that new price. Estimate the cost of one drum first. Then find the exact cost. A crew member says the two changes add up to 17% off $300, or $249. Is that right?"],key:[`11.75 feet, or 11${fr(3,4)} feet. The 5 lines use 5 × 7.4 = 37 feet. Left: 48.75 − 37 = 11.75. Look for changing 48${fr(3,4)} to 48.75, or 7.4 to 7${fr(2,5)}. Or: work it all in fractions, 48 3/4 − 37 = 11 3/4. Partial: 37 feet of rope used found with no subtraction is approaching. (7.EE.B.3, converting between forms in a multi-step problem)`,"189 pounds. Estimate: about 3.5 × 6 is about 21 pounds a day, and 20 × 9 is about 180 pounds. Exact: 3.5 × 6 = 21 pounds a day, and 21 × 9 = 189 pounds. Yes, 189 is close to the estimate of about 180, so it makes sense. Needs an estimate, the exact weight, and the check. Or: 3.5 × 9 = 31.5 pounds for one member, then × 6. Partial: two of the three parts given is approaching. (7.EE.B.3, multi-step problem and reasonableness)","$243. Estimate: $300 raised a little is a bit over $300, and a quarter off that is a bit under $250. Exact: the raise is 0.08 × 300 = 24, so the new price is $324. The discount is 0.25 × 324 = 81, so 324 − 81 = $243. Also correct: 0.75 × 324 = 243. No, the crew member is not right. Percents of different amounts cannot be added that way: the 8% is taken on $300 and the 25% is taken on $324, so the answer is $243, not $249. Partial: $324 given as the cost leaves the discount out and is approaching. (7.EE.B.3, multi-step percents and assessing reasonableness)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Depot Prices",short:"Depot Prices",parts:[{std:["7.RP.A.3"],dur:260,dir:"Solve on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer. Go in order, because they get harder.",desc:"multistep percent problems (markdown, a fee, percent decrease, simple interest, percent error, and finding the original price)",html:()=>lead("The supply depot keeps the expedition's prices and records. Work through all six."),qs:["A parka is priced at $180. At the end of the season the depot takes 35% off. What is the sale price?","A charter flight to base camp costs $1,250 plus a 6% landing fee. What is the total cost?","Two winters ago the bay held 1,450 square miles of sea ice in March. This March it held 1,160 square miles. What is the percent decrease?","The expedition borrows $4,000 for new radios at 3% simple interest for 2 years. How much interest does it owe? What is the total it pays back?","A pilot estimated the ice runway was 1,900 feet long. Measured with a tape, it was 2,000 feet. What is the percent error of the estimate?","After a 20% discount, a used snow machine cost $1,360. What was the price before the discount?"],key:["$117. Discount: 0.35 × 180 = 63. Sale price: 180 − 63 = 117. Also correct: 0.65 × 180 = 117. Partial: $63 found with no sale price is approaching. (7.RP.A.3, markdown)","$1,325. Fee: 0.06 × 1,250 = 75. Total: 1,250 + 75 = 1,325. Also correct: 1.06 × 1,250 = 1,325. Partial: $75 found with no total is approaching. (7.RP.A.3, a fee)","20% decrease. Change: 1,450 − 1,160 = 290. Then 290 ÷ 1,450 = 0.20. Watch for dividing by 1,160 instead of the original 1,450. Or: 1,160 ÷ 1,450 = 0.80, so the new area is 80% of the old. (7.RP.A.3, percent decrease)","Interest: $240. Total paid back: $4,240. I = 4,000 × 0.03 × 2 = 240, and 4,000 + 240 = 4,240. Watch for stopping at one year, which gives $120. Or: 3% of 4,000 is 120 for one year, so two years is 240. Partial: $240 found with no total paid back is approaching. (7.RP.A.3, simple interest)","5%. The estimate was off by 2,000 − 1,900 = 100 feet. Then 100 ÷ 2,000 = 0.05. Divide by the measured length, not by the estimate. Partial: about 5.3% from dividing by 1,900 does not count. (7.RP.A.3, percent error)","$1,700. The sale price is 80% of the old price, so 0.80p = 1,360 and p = 1,360 ÷ 0.80 = 1,700. Check: 0.20 × 1,700 = 340, and 1,700 − 340 = 1,360. Watch for adding 20% of 1,360, which gives 1,632 and is wrong. Or: the $1,360 is four fifths of the old price, so one fifth is 340 and the whole is 1,700. (7.RP.A.3, finding the original amount)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Field Notebook",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"argument",label:"Argument",icon:"⚖️",hint:"Camp on the floe or on the shore?"},std:["W.7.1"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an argument writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">The expedition has to pick one spot for base camp. Should it camp on the ice floe or on the rocky shore? Write an argument of 5 to 7 sentences. Use the fact box.</p>${factBox("Fact box: two places to put base camp",["<strong>On the ice floe.</strong> A floe is a wide slab of floating sea ice. The camp would sit right next to the drilling sites, so there is no travel time.","A floe drifts up to 2 miles a day, and a crack can force the whole camp to move in under an hour. Last season a floe camp had to move twice in eight weeks.","<strong>On the rocky shore.</strong> The ground is solid, and the supply plane can land on the gravel strip beside camp.","The drilling sites are 9 miles away, a 3-hour trip each way by snow machine. Shore crews lost 11 working days to travel in the same eight weeks."])}${bullets(["State your claim in the first sentence.","Give at least two reasons, each with evidence from the fact box.","Tell what someone on the other side would say, and answer it.","Link your ideas with words such as because, however, and for example.","Keep a formal style, with no slang.","End with a conclusion that follows from your reasons."])}`,key:["Opens with a clear claim and puts the reasons in an order that makes sense (W.7.1, claim and organization)","Names what the other side would claim and answers it (W.7.1, opposing claims)","Backs the claim with logical reasons and relevant evidence from the fact box, such as the 2 miles of drift a day, the two moves in eight weeks, the 9-mile trip, or the 11 lost working days (W.7.1, reasons and evidence)","Links claim, reasons, and evidence with words, phrases, or clauses such as because, however, for example (W.7.1, cohesion)","Holds a formal style with no slang or texting language (W.7.1, formal style)","Closes with a statement that follows from the argument (W.7.1, conclusion)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"informative",label:"Informative",icon:"📘",hint:"How a polar camp gets drinking water"},std:["W.7.2"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Explain how a polar camp gets drinking water when everything around it is frozen. Write 5 to 7 sentences. Use the fact box.</p>${factBox("Fact box: water at a polar camp",["Sea ice forms from salty ocean water, but freezing pushes most of the salt out. Ice that is a year or more old is nearly fresh.","Crews look for old floe ice, which has a blue tint, or for clean snow. New gray ice is still too salty to drink.","Ice and snow have to be melted on a stove. About 10 pots of loose snow melt down to 1 pot of water, so crews pack the pot tight.","Melting costs fuel, so a camp budgets fuel for water the way it budgets food.","Melted water is poured through a cloth to catch grit, and crews never cut snow from near the dog lines or the fuel drums."])}${bullets(["Introduce the topic and tell what you will explain.","Put the facts in a clear order, in your own sentences.","Use linking words such as first, after that, and as a result.","Use exact words such as floe ice, melt, fuel, and filter.","Keep a formal style.","End with a concluding sentence."])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and previews what will follow (W.7.2, introduction)","Groups the ideas on a clear plan, such as the steps in order or finding the ice and then melting it (W.7.2, organization)","Develops the topic with relevant facts, definitions, details, or examples from the fact box, in the student's own sentences (W.7.2, development)","Uses transitions to connect ideas, such as first, after that, because, as a result (W.7.2, transitions)","Uses precise words and topic vocabulary in a formal style (W.7.2, precise language and formal style)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from the explanation (W.7.2, conclusion)"]}]},{id:9,title:"The Instrument and the Boot",short:"Story Theme",parts:[{std:["RL.7.2"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"a theme of a story, how it develops, and an objective summary",html:()=>block(STORY_TITLE,STORY),qs:["What is a theme of this story? Say it as a sentence about an idea, not a sentence about Ines.","How does that theme develop over the course of the story? Point to two moments.","Give an objective summary of the story in 2 or 3 sentences."],key:["Something like: a measurement and a trained sense each tell you something the other cannot, so a good record keeps both. Also acceptable: a number can be exactly right and still not be the whole truth. A plot sentence such as “Ines learns from Pavel” names what happens, not a theme. (RL.7.2, theme)","Any two moments: at the start the gauge gives a clean number in four seconds and Ines underlines it for the board, which sets the number up as all there is; in the middle Pavel stamps and says the gauge reads how far down the ice goes but not what it is made of, which splits the two kinds of knowing apart; near the end Ines does not cross the number out but writes a second line under it, which joins them; and Pavel's last line, that she will learn faster because she has the gauge to argue with, states the idea outright. (RL.7.2, development over the text)","Covers the whole story with no opinion, for example: A radio operator takes a thickness reading of 162 centimeters and records it as good news. An older observer, who judges the ice by sound and color, scrapes the snow away and shows her that the same thick ice is gray and full of holes. She keeps her number and adds a second line describing what the ice looks and sounds like. Leaves out judgments such as “Pavel was right all along.” (RL.7.2, objective summary)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Two Ways of Knowing",short:"Two Views",parts:[{std:["RL.7.6"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Use the story. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing how an author develops and contrasts the points of view of two characters",html:()=>lookBackStory(),qs:["What is Ines's point of view about how to judge the ice? What is Pavel's?","Name two things the author does to build the contrast between them.","Whose point of view is the story told through? How would the story change if it were told through the other one?"],key:["Ines trusts what can be measured and written down: the gauge, the number, the log the board will read. Pavel trusts what twenty years of walking has taught him: the sound under a heel, the color under the snow. Neither is presented as foolish. (RL.7.6, points of view of different characters)","Any two: the two are introduced in parallel opening sentences, one with the gauge and one with his heel; Pavel is given almost no words, while Ines is given the thinking, so his certainty reads as settled and hers as working; the author puts their methods in direct conflict over one spot, where both are correct about different things; and the concrete detail is split between them, the green light and the four seconds on one side, the gray porous ice on the other. (RL.7.6, how the contrast is developed)","Through Ines. The reader sees her write the number, watch Pavel, and decide what to add to the log, and the reader never learns what Pavel is thinking. Told through Pavel, the gauge would be background and the story would be about watching someone learn, so the moment of change at the log would be seen from outside instead of felt from inside. (RL.7.6, narrator's point of view)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Story and History",short:"Fiction vs History",parts:[{std:["RL.7.9"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the history excerpt. You have already read the story. Then answer out loud.",desc:"comparing a fictional portrayal of a time and place with a historical account of the same period",html:()=>`${oneText(HIST_TITLE,HIST_SRC,HIST)}${lookBackStory()}`,qs:["What does the history excerpt say observers did between the test holes? Where do you see that same practice in the story?","Name one thing the story shows that the history excerpt only states.","The history says the old notes were written underneath the new numbers. How does the story use that fact, and what does the fiction add that the history does not?"],key:["They read the ice itself: the color of the surface where the wind had swept the snow away, the sound a boot made on it, and the way a sledge runner tracked. In the story Pavel stamps his heel every eight or ten steps and listens, and he scrapes the snow away to look at the color of the ice. (RL.7.9, same period in both texts)","The history states that observers used sound and color. The story shows it happening: the three stamps in one spot, the glove scraping the snow, the gray ice full of small holes like a sponge. Also acceptable: the history states that the two kinds of evidence sat side by side in the logs, and the story shows one entry being written that way. (RL.7.9, fiction showing what history states)","The story turns that one sentence into the ending. Ines does not cross out 162; she writes gray, porous, sounds dull underfoot underneath it, which is exactly the practice the history describes. What the fiction adds is a reason and a feeling for it: a person who wanted the number to be the whole answer, an older observer who says almost nothing, and the twenty years it took him. A history can record what was done. The story gives a reader someone to be while it happens. (RL.7.9, how fiction uses history)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Camp or Buoys",short:"Two Authors",parts:[{std:["RI.7.9"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the column. You have already read the article about drift camps. Then answer out loud using both.",desc:"analyzing how two authors on the same topic emphasize different evidence and advance different interpretations",html:()=>`${oneText(BUOY_TITLE,BUOY_SRC,BUOY)}${lookBack("Look back at the drift camp article",floeBlock())}`,qs:["Both texts are about the same question. State that question in your own words.","What evidence does each author put in front of the reader? Name the evidence that only one of them uses.","The two authors agree on one fact and read it in opposite ways. What fact, and what does each one make of it?"],key:["How the thickness of sea ice and the water underneath it should be measured, and whether a staffed camp on the ice is still needed to do it. Any wording of that question. (RI.7.9, same topic)","The article emphasizes what satellites cannot do: they photograph the surface and measure how far the ice spreads, but they cannot give thickness and cannot reach the water below, and it describes the drill, the tape, and the instrument on a cable. Farrell emphasizes cost and coverage: four salaries, a chartered aircraft, nine tons of supplies, a buoy for the price of one flight, readings every six hours for up to three years, 214 buoys reporting from more positions in a week than the stations managed in a decade, and drones running 40 kilometers from one hole. Only Farrell gives costs and counts. Only the article describes how the measurement is actually taken from the ice. (RI.7.9, emphasizing different evidence)","Both agree that thickness and the water below can only be measured from the ice itself and not from above. The article reads that as the reason the camp has to exist, so the risk is the price of the data. Farrell reads the same fact as an argument about equipment rather than about people: something still has to be down there, but a buoy frozen into the floe is down there too, for three years, and never has to be flown off. (RI.7.9, different interpretations of the same facts)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Unfamiliar Words",short:"Word Work",parts:[{std:["L.7.4"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Use the texts in front of you. Answer out loud and say which strategy you used.",desc:"working out unknown and multiple-meaning words from context, word parts, and a reference",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Three words from the texts in this game.</p>${bullets(["Pavel calls the ice <strong>rotten</strong>.","Dr. Farrell writes about an <strong>autonomous</strong> buoy.","The column says drones run a <strong>line of readings</strong>, which scientists call a <strong>profile</strong>."])}`,qs:["What does Pavel mean by rotten ice? Which words around it told you?","Autonomous is built from two parts: auto, meaning self, and nomos, meaning rule. Work out what an autonomous buoy is, and check your meaning against the column.","Profile is a multiple-meaning word. What does it mean in this column, and how would you check that you picked the right meaning?"],key:["Ice that is thick but weak, gray and wet looking and full of small holes like a sponge, so it will not hold the way solid ice would. The clues are in the same paragraph: the description of the gray, porous surface, and Pavel's line that the gauge says how far down the ice goes but not what it is made of. Not rotten the way food is. (L.7.4, context clues)","A buoy that rules itself, that is, one that works on its own with nobody operating it. The column confirms it: the buoy freezes into the floe and reports every six hours for as long as three years, including winters when no camp is staffed, and it needs no crack watch. (L.7.4, word parts, confirmed in context)","Here a profile is a set of measurements taken in a line, out from the hole or down through the water, so the readings together show how a quantity changes along that line. The other common meanings are a side view of a face and a short account of a person, and neither fits a drone under the ice. To check, look the word up and read all the numbered senses, then pick the one that fits the sentence, rather than taking the first one listed. (L.7.4, multiple-meaning word and using a reference)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Your Turn on the Ice",short:"Narrative",parts:[{std:["W.7.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative with an orientation, a clear event sequence, dialogue or description, and a conclusion",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write the scene of your first hour as the new ice observer, the morning after Pavel scraped the snow away and showed you the gray ice. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${pills(["Where you are","What you try","What someone says","What you write in the log"])}${bullets(["Set the scene and say who is there","Put the events in an order a reader can follow","Use dialogue or description to make one moment real","Use precise words and details a reader can see, hear, or feel","End in a way that follows from what happened"])}`,key:["Orients the reader: the situation, the narrator, and who else is there (W.7.3, orientation)","Events unfold in a logical, well-structured sequence (W.7.3, event sequence)","Uses dialogue, pacing, or description to develop an event or a character (W.7.3, technique)","Uses precise words and relevant descriptive details, such as the sound underfoot or the color under the snow (W.7.3, descriptive detail)","Ends with a conclusion that follows from the narrated events (W.7.3, conclusion)","The writing should fit the world of the story: a drift station, a thickness gauge, a log. Note anything that contradicts it."]}]},{id:15,title:"Check the Sources",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.7.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:70,dir:"Your research question is on the screen. Say numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Write numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"judging sources, spotting plagiarism, paraphrasing with credit, and writing the source information",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Research question: do staffed drift camps still collect readings that instruments alone cannot?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:12px 0">${srcCard("Source 1","Explainer article, “Camp on a Moving Floor,” in this game. Describes how thickness and the water below are measured, and what satellites cannot do.")}${srcCard("Source 2","Opinion column, “Instruments Instead of Tents,” by Dr. Ngozi Farrell, Polar Instrument Review, 2026. Gives costs and counts for buoys and drones.")}${srcCard("Source 3","Forum post, no author, no date: “My cousin worked up there once and said the camps are just for show now.”")}</div><div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">${srcCard("Student note A","A drift camp costs a season of four salaries, a chartered aircraft, and roughly nine tons of fuel and supplies flown onto moving ice.")}${srcCard("Student note B","Farrell puts the cost of a camp at four salaries plus a charter flight and about nine tons of supplies, against a buoy at roughly one flight.")}${srcCard("Student note C","Farrell calls a buoy the price of “one charter flight.”")}</div>`,qs:["Which two sources would you use for this question, and why is the third one not usable?","One of the three student notes is plagiarism. Which one, and how would you fix it?","Paraphrase, in your own words and with credit, what Farrell says a buoy reports and for how long.","Write Source 2 the way you would list it at the end of a report: author, title, where it was published, and the year."],key:["Sources 1 and 2. Source 1 explains how the measurements are actually taken and what satellites cannot reach, and Source 2 is signed, dated, published in a field review, and gives checkable figures, even though it argues one side. Source 3 has no author and no date, it is hearsay passed through a relative, and it offers no evidence at all, so nothing in it can be checked. (W.7.8, assessing credibility)","Note A. It copies Farrell's sentence almost word for word with no quotation marks and no credit. Fix it either by quoting it exactly inside quotation marks and naming Farrell, or by rewriting it in new words and naming Farrell, which is what Note B does. Note C is a correct short quotation with credit. (W.7.8, avoiding plagiarism)","A real paraphrase that names the author, such as: Farrell says a buoy frozen into the floe sends back the thickness of the ice and the temperature and saltiness of the water every six hours, and can keep doing it for up to three years, including the winters when nobody is at camp. Must be in the student's own words and must credit Farrell or the column. (W.7.8, paraphrase with citation)","Ngozi Farrell, “Instruments Instead of Tents,” Polar Instrument Review, 2026. Any order is fine as long as all four pieces are there. (W.7.8, standard citation information)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Make the Case",short:"Present",parts:[{std:["SL.7.4"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:"Plan for 30 seconds. Then speak for 45 to 60 seconds. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"presenting a claim with salient points, pertinent facts, and clear delivery",html:()=>`<p class="big">The board has to decide: fund one more season of the staffed drift camp, or spend the same money on buoys and drones. Make your case.</p>${pills(["My claim","The strongest point","The facts behind it","So the board should"])}${bullets(["Open with your claim","Lead with your strongest point, not your longest one","Back each point with a fact or an example from the two texts","Look at the camera, speak up, and say the words clearly"])}`,key:["States a clear claim and finishes with it (SL.7.4, presenting a claim)","Emphasizes the salient points in a focused, coherent order rather than retelling both texts (SL.7.4, salient points)","Uses pertinent facts, details, and examples from the texts, such as the 214 buoys, the three years of reporting, the instrument lowered on a cable, or what satellites cannot measure (SL.7.4, pertinent evidence)","Uses appropriate eye contact, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation for 45 to 60 seconds (SL.7.4, delivery)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Draw the Triangle",short:"Triangles",parts:[{std:["7.G.A.2"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Use a ruler and a protractor on your paper. Sketch each one or explain why you cannot. Hold your paper up.",mats:["a ruler","a protractor"],desc:"drawing triangles from given measures and deciding whether the conditions give one triangle, many, or none",qs:["Try to draw a triangle with sides 4 cm, 5 cm and 12 cm. What happens, and why?","Draw a triangle with sides 6 cm, 8 cm and 10 cm. Is there more than one triangle that fits those three measures?","Draw a triangle with angles 40, 60 and 80 degrees. How many different triangles fit that condition?"],key:["No triangle is possible. The two shorter sides add to 4 + 5 = 9 centimeters, which is less than the 12 centimeter side, so they cannot reach across to meet. Three lengths make a triangle only when the two shorter ones add to more than the longest one. Partial: saying none with no comparison of 4 + 5 against 12 is approaching. (7.G.A.2, no triangle)","Exactly one. Three given side lengths fix the triangle completely, so every triangle a student draws with those sides is the same one turned or flipped. This one has a right angle, because 6 squared plus 8 squared equals 10 squared. Partial: exactly one with no reason that three sides fix the triangle is approaching. (7.G.A.2, a unique triangle)","Infinitely many. The three angles add to 180, so the condition can be met, but the angles fix only the shape and not the size. Every triangle that fits is a scaled copy of every other one, large or small. Partial: more than one with nothing said about size not being fixed is approaching. (7.G.A.2, more than one triangle)"]}]},{id:18,title:"The Drum Lid",short:"Circles",parts:[{std:["7.G.B.4"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work on your paper. Use 22/7 for pi. Hold your work up and say each answer.",desc:"circumference and area of a circle, and an informal derivation of the relationship between them",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${lidFig()}</div>`,qs:["The lid has a diameter of 56 centimeters. Find its circumference.","Find the area of the lid.","Explain informally why the area of a circle is half the circumference times the radius."],key:["176 centimeters. C = pi × d = 22/7 × 56 = 22 × 8 = 176. Or: C = 2 pi r with r = 28. (7.G.B.4, circumference)","2,464 square centimeters. The radius is 28 centimeters, so A = pi × r squared = 22/7 × 784 = 22 × 112 = 2,464. Partial: using 56 as the radius gives 9,856 and does not count. (7.G.B.4, area)","Cut the circle into many thin wedges and lay them out alternately point up and point down. They form a shape close to a rectangle. The long side is made of the arcs from half the wedges, which is half the circumference, and the short side is the radius. So the area is half the circumference times the radius, and since the circumference is 2 pi r, that gives pi r squared. The thinner the wedges, the closer the shape comes to a true rectangle. Partial: naming the rectangle with no account of which side is the radius is approaching. (7.G.B.4, informal derivation)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Crate and Floor",short:"Area & Volume",parts:[{std:["7.G.B.6"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up and say each answer with its units.",desc:"volume and surface area of a right prism, and the area of a composite two-dimensional figure",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${floorPlanFig()}</div>`,qs:["A supply crate is a rectangular box 1.2 meters long, 0.8 meters wide and 0.5 meters high. Find its volume.","Find the surface area of that same crate.","The floor plan shows the tent floor. Find its total area."],key:["0.48 cubic meters. V = 1.2 × 0.8 × 0.5. Or: V = Bh with B = 0.96 square meters. (7.G.B.6, volume of a right prism)","3.92 square meters. The three different faces have areas 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96, 1.2 × 0.5 = 0.6 and 0.8 × 0.5 = 0.4. Each one appears twice, so the surface area is 2 × (0.96 + 0.6 + 0.4) = 2 × 1.96 = 3.92. Or: work out all six faces one at a time and add. Partial: 1.96 for three faces only is approaching. (7.G.B.6, surface area)","14.1 square meters. The rectangle is 4 × 3 = 12 square meters. The vestibule triangle has a base of 3 meters and a height of 1.4 meters, so its area is 1/2 × 3 × 1.4 = 2.1 square meters. Together, 12 + 2.1 = 14.1. Partial: 12 for the rectangle alone, or 2.1 for the triangle alone, is approaching. (7.G.B.6, composite figure)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Two Sites",short:"Compare Data",parts:[{std:["7.SP.B.4"],n:3,dur:65,dir:"Look at the table. Work on your paper and say each answer.",desc:"using center and variability from two random samples to draw an informal comparative inference",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${siteTable()}</div>`,qs:["Find the range of each sample. What does the difference between the two ranges tell you?","The mean absolute deviation is 3.8 centimeters for Site A and 15.8 centimeters for Site B. The two means differ by 1.0 centimeter. How many of Site A's mean absolute deviations is that difference?","What comparative inference can you draw about the two sites? Why does it matter that these were random samples?"],key:["Site A: 161 − 147 = 14 centimeters. Site B: 182 − 128 = 54 centimeters. Site B's readings are spread almost four times as wide, so the ice at Site B is far less consistent from point to point even though the two sites average about the same. Partial: both ranges right with nothing said about consistency is approaching. (7.SP.B.4, measures of variability)","About 0.26 of one mean absolute deviation, since 1.0 ÷ 3.8 is about 0.26. The gap between the two centers is only about a quarter of the ordinary spread at Site A, which is small. A difference much smaller than one mean absolute deviation is not a meaningful difference in the centers. Partial: 0.26 worked out with nothing said about the difference being small is approaching. (7.SP.B.4, comparing a difference in centers to variability)","The two sites have about the same typical thickness, around 154 or 155 centimeters, but Site B is much less predictable, so a route planned across Site B would need many more test holes before anyone could trust it. The samples were taken at randomly chosen drill points, which is what lets ten readings stand in for the whole site. Ten holes picked by eye, or picked where the ice looked best, would say nothing about the site as a whole. Partial: comparing the two sites with nothing said about why random drill points matter is approaching. (7.SP.B.4, informal comparative inference from random samples)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Two Mornings",short:"Compound Events",parts:[{std:["7.SP.C.8"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Work on your paper. Draw the list or the tree, hold it up, and say each answer.",desc:"probabilities of compound events using an organized list, a tree diagram, and a simulation",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Each morning the crew starts at one of two drill sites, A or B, chosen by a coin flip. Separately, the camp log shows the generator starts on the first pull 3 mornings out of every 4. The two things do not affect each other.</p>`,qs:["Make an organized list or a table of all the outcomes for one morning. Then find the probability of starting at Site A on a morning when the generator starts on the first pull.","Draw a tree diagram for two mornings of generator starts. What is the probability it starts on the first pull on both mornings?","Describe a simulation you could run to estimate the probability that the generator starts on the first pull on at least one of two mornings. Then give the exact probability."],key:["3/8. The four outcomes are A with a first-pull start, A without, B with, B without. The site is 1/2 either way and a first-pull start is 3/4, so A with a first-pull start is 1/2 × 3/4 = 3/8. A correct list or two-by-two table has to be shown. Or: an area model split into 8 equal parts, 3 of which are A with a first-pull start. Partial: the list or table right with no probability is approaching. (7.SP.C.8, organized list or table)","9/16. The tree has two branches at the first morning, first pull with probability 3/4 and not with probability 1/4, and the same two branches again at the second morning. The both-first-pull path is 3/4 × 3/4 = 9/16. Or: 0.75 × 0.75 = 0.5625 in decimals. Partial: the tree drawn right with no product is approaching. (7.SP.C.8, tree diagram)","Any sound simulation, such as: make a spinner with four equal sections, three marked starts and one marked no. Spin it twice to stand for two mornings, and write down whether at least one spin came up starts. Repeat the pair of spins many times, say 100, and divide the number of successful trials by the number of trials. Drawing from four cards, three marked starts, with the card replaced each time, works just as well. The exact probability is 15/16, because the only way to fail is for both mornings to miss, and 1/4 × 1/4 = 1/16, so 1 − 1/16 = 15/16. Partial: a simulation described with no exact probability, or the exact probability with no simulation, is approaching. (7.SP.C.8, simulation)"]}]},{id:22,title:"What Outlasted the Empire",short:"Outlasted",parts:[{std:["HSS-7.1"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing which Roman institutions outlasted the western empire and why they did",html:()=>`<p class="big">A reading in the ship’s library lists what happened to four Roman things after the western government stopped functioning.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Roman thing</th><th>After the west fell</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the Latin language</td><td>kept as the written language of law, record and learning for a thousand years, while spoken Latin split into the languages of Spain, France, Portugal, Italy and Romania</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Roman written law</td><td>collected and reissued in the east, largely forgotten in the west, where the Church kept a law of its own built on Roman models, then recovered and studied again from about 1100</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the long distance road and sea network</td><td>roads used but little repaired; the volume of trade across the Mediterranean fell sharply</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the big tax collecting bureaucracy</td><td>gone in the west within a few generations</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("bridge",74)}${ico("map",74)}</div>`,qs:["Two of these four survived in the west and two did not. What do the two survivors have in common?","Spoken Latin turned into several different languages while written Latin stayed the same for centuries. Explain how both of those things can be true at once.","The tax bureaucracy disappeared first. Explain why that is what a government loses when it stops being able to govern.","Roman law was recovered and studied again from about 1100. Why would lawyers and rulers of that time go looking for the law of a state that no longer existed?"],key:["They are both written and portable, and they can be kept alive by a small number of trained people copying and teaching, which in the west meant the clergy. A road network and a tax service need large organizations, constant money and many workers, so they stop the moment the state that paid for them stops. What survives a collapse is what is cheap to carry and can be held by a few. Partial: naming what survived with no contrast to what needed a large organization is approaching. (HSS-7.1)","A spoken language drifts because every generation changes it a little and regions drift apart once travel and contact fall away. A written language can be frozen because it is learned from books and taught to a rule, so its users keep copying a fixed standard rather than following what people actually say. The writing stopped being anyone’s mother tongue, and that is exactly why it stopped changing. Partial: one of the two halves explained is approaching. (HSS-7.1)","A bureaucracy costs money to run and can only be paid for out of the taxes it collects, so the moment collection fails the officials are not paid and go home. It is also the part of government that needs the whole machine working: records, courts, roads, soldiers to compel payment. Once it is gone a ruler can only live off his own lands and the loyalty of men who hold land, which is a completely different kind of state. Partial: naming the cost with nothing said about what kind of state is left is approaching. (HSS-7.1)","Because trade, towns and kingdoms had grown complicated again and the customary local law was not built to handle contracts, property, inheritance and disputes across a distance. Roman law was a worked out, written system that already dealt with those questions, and it gave rulers a body of law not controlled by local lords. A ready made system that fits a new need will be dug up and used. Partial: saying it was admired with no new need named is approaching. (HSS-7.1)"]},{std:["HSS-7.6"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing what the building of a great cathedral required of a medieval town and of the Church",html:()=>`<p class="big">A cathedral is the church of a bishop, and in the 1100s and 1200s the Catholic Church and the towns of France and England built them on a scale never attempted before. One could take sixty to a hundred and fifty years. The money came from the bishop’s estates and tithe income, gifts from nobles and merchants, the offerings of pilgrims who came to see the relics of a saint kept there, and levies on the town. The work needed quarrymen, carters, masons, carpenters, glaziers and metalworkers, a master mason who planned and directed it, and lodgings, food and tools for all of them, year after year. Many were begun and never finished to the original design, and several were redesigned halfway through as the master mason changed.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bridge",74)}${ico("lantern",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["A hundred years is longer than anyone working on it would live. Name two problems that length of project creates.","The building needed masons, glaziers and metalworkers for decades. What would that do to the town’s economy?","The money came from the bishop’s lands, from nobles, from merchants, from pilgrims and from the town. Explain what that tells you about who held wealth in medieval Europe."],key:["Any two: nobody alive at the start sees the end, so the plan must survive in drawings, records and trained workers rather than in one head; the money has to be raised again by each generation and can dry up; the design goes out of fashion or the next master mason wants to change it; and the half built structure has to stand and be usable in the meantime. Partial: one problem named is approaching. (HSS-7.6)","It brought in skilled workers who settled, spent their wages locally and trained apprentices, so crafts and guilds grew. It created steady demand for stone, timber, iron, lead, glass, food and lodging, and drew carting trade. And the finished building drew pilgrims who had to be housed and fed, which is a lasting source of income. A great cathedral site was an industry, and the relics were the reason the visitors came. Partial: one economic effect named is approaching. (HSS-7.6)","That no single party could pay for it: the Church had estates and the tithe, the nobles had rents, the merchants had money from trade, the pilgrims brought offerings, and the townspeople could be levied. Wealth was spread across a landholding Church, a landed nobility and a rising commercial class, and a project this size needed all of them, which also meant all of them had a claim on how it looked and whose name went on it. Partial: listing the contributors with nothing said about what that shows about where wealth sat is approaching. (HSS-7.6)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Healers, Griots and Compasses",short:"Healers",parts:[{std:["HSS-7.2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Islamic charitable endowments, hospitals, trained physicians and medical writing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Giving to the poor is one of the five duties of a Muslim, and Islamic law also recognized the waqf, a permanent religious endowment: a wealthy founder signed over land or shops, and the rent paid for a named purpose forever and could not be sold or seized. From the 800s, waqf endowments paid for large hospitals in cities such as Baghdad, Damascus and Cairo, as well as for mosques, schools, wells and inns for travellers. The hospitals treated patients without charge, kept separate wards, had storerooms of prepared medicines, and trained students beside working physicians. Physicians were examined before they could practice. One of them, Ibn Sina, wrote a medical encyclopedia that organized everything then known into a single arrangement; it was translated into Latin and taught in European universities for about five hundred years.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("herb",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["A founder signed over shops and land whose rent paid for the hospital, rather than giving a sum of money. Explain why the endowment is the stronger arrangement.","Physicians were examined before they could practice, and students trained beside working doctors. Name one thing each of those two rules protects against.","Ibn Sina’s book was used in Europe for centuries. Name one thing a single well organized encyclopedia does that a shelf of separate treatises does not."],key:["A sum of money is spent and then gone, and it loses value as prices rise. Rent producing property keeps paying every year, keeps roughly up with prices, and in law could not be sold or taken for anything else, so the hospital outlived its founder and did not depend on each year’s donations or on the ruler’s goodwill. A religious duty to give, plus a legal form that made the gift permanent, is what funded public provision in these cities. Partial: naming the rents with nothing said about outliving the founder is approaching. (HSS-7.2)","An examination keeps out people who have not learned the subject, so a patient is not at the mercy of anyone who calls himself a doctor. Training beside working physicians makes sure the knowledge is practical and not only from books, so a newly qualified doctor has actually seen patients. One guards the standard, the other guards the skill. Partial: one of the two requirements explained is approaching. (HSS-7.2)","It lets a reader find a topic, compare treatments and see how the parts fit together, and it gives a teacher a syllabus to teach in order. It also fixes a shared vocabulary, so physicians in different countries mean the same thing by the same word, which is what makes a field teachable and arguable rather than a collection of local practice. Partial: saying it was useful with nothing said about a shared vocabulary or a teachable order is approaching. (HSS-7.2)"]},{std:["HSS-7.4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the griot and oral record keeping in Mali, and how it compares with written records",html:()=>`<p class="big">In Mali and the kingdoms around it, trained specialists called griots held the history. A griot learned long accounts by heart from a master over many years: the descent of ruling families, the founding of the kingdom, the deeds of its heroes, the agreements between clans. They performed at court and at public occasions, often with an instrument, and were consulted when a question of descent or of precedent had to be settled. Written records in Arabic also existed in the trading and scholarly towns, kept by a different group of people for different purposes.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("megaphone",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["A griot was consulted when a question of descent had to be settled. Explain what position that gives him in the society.","Name one strength of an oral record and one strength of a written one.","Griots performed in public and at court. What does performing an account in front of an audience do to how accurate it stays?"],key:["It makes him an officer of the political system, not an entertainer: if the right to rule or to inherit depends on descent, then the person who holds the authoritative account of descent is part of deciding who gets power. That gives him standing, protection and influence, and it also means rulers have an interest in what he says. Partial: calling him a storyteller with nothing said about descent deciding power is approaching. (HSS-7.4)","Oral: it needs nothing but trained people, so it survives where paper does not; it can be delivered to a whole community including people who cannot read; and it carries tone, music and emphasis that writing loses. Written: it does not change when the person who held it dies, it can be checked word for word years later, and it can be sent somewhere without the author. Partial: one of the oral and the written strengths given is approaching. (HSS-7.4)","It works both ways, and a good answer says so. A public performance before people who have heard the account before, including rivals whose ancestors are in it, is a check: a griot who changed it would be contradicted. But performing for a patron also creates pressure to flatter the family paying, and there is no earlier copy to compare against. Accept either side with reasoning. Partial: one side of the argument given is approaching. (HSS-7.4)"]},{std:["HSS-7.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the compass, shipbuilding and the great Chinese maritime voyages",html:()=>`<p class="big">Chinese sailors were using a magnetized needle to find direction by the 1000s, which let a ship hold a course out of sight of land and in cloud. Chinese shipyards built sea going ships with several masts, a stern mounted rudder, and hulls divided into watertight compartments. Between 1405 and 1433 the government sent out seven huge fleets under the admiral Zheng He, which reached Southeast Asia, India, Arabia and the east coast of Africa, carrying envoys and trade goods. Then the voyages were stopped by decision of the court and the fleets were not rebuilt.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("compass",74)}${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("anchor",74)}</div>`,qs:["A hull divided into watertight compartments is a design choice. Explain the advantage at sea.","A compass lets a ship hold a course in cloud and out of sight of land. Name two things that makes possible that coastal sailing does not.","The voyages were stopped by a decision at court rather than by any failure at sea. Name one reason a government might end a program that was working."],key:["If the hull is holed, only the compartment that is breached fills, so the ship stays afloat and can be repaired instead of sinking. It also lets cargo be loaded and separated by compartment. It is a design that accepts damage will happen and plans for it. Partial: naming the compartments with nothing said about the ship staying afloat is approaching. (HSS-7.3)","Any two: sailing direct across open water instead of following the coast, which is much shorter; keeping course at night and in weather, so a voyage is not halted for days; sailing where there is no coast to follow at all; and planning a route and a timetable in advance, since direction no longer depends on what can be seen. Partial: one change named is approaching. (HSS-7.3)","Any sound reason: the cost was enormous and the treasury had other demands, including a land frontier that looked more dangerous than the sea; officials argued the voyages brought back prestige and curiosities rather than revenue; a faction at court that had backed them lost power; and a government that had decided its attention belonged inland had no reason to keep paying. The point is that the capability existed and was given up by choice. Partial: saying they could not do it any more is the misreading to watch for; the capability was given up by choice, so that answer is approaching. (HSS-7.3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Side by Side",short:"Side by Side",parts:[{std:["HSS-7.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing how two religious traditions in Japan occupied different roles rather than replacing one another",html:()=>`<p class="big">Japan’s older tradition, later called Shinto, was tied to particular places: a mountain, a spring, a grove or a village had its own kami, the spirits or powers of that place, honored at a shrine with a gateway and seasonal observances carried out by the local community. Buddhism arrived from the mainland in the 500s with texts, trained specialists, monastic houses and a body of learning. The two did not drive each other out. Over centuries most families took part in both, and the two became associated with different parts of life: local, seasonal and village matters on one side, and questions about death and what follows it on the other. Shrines and monastic houses were often built on the same site.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pine",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}</div>`,qs:["One tradition is tied to particular places and the other arrived with texts and trained specialists. Explain why those two kinds of thing do not have to compete.","Over time the two became associated with different parts of life. Name one practical advantage that arrangement has for the people living with it.","A tradition tied to local places and seasons, and one with texts, schools and houses that own land. Name one difference that would make to a government dealing with each."],key:["A tradition rooted in a named mountain or spring makes claims about that place and that community; it has nothing to say about the subjects a textual tradition addresses, and it cannot be exported to another village in any case. A textual tradition travels, teaches and explains, and does not displace the spring. They answer different questions, so a person can use both without choosing. Partial: saying people followed both with no account of the different questions they answer is approaching. (HSS-7.5)","Nobody has to give anything up. The village keeps its own observances, which are also what holds the village together, and still has somewhere to turn for the questions its own tradition does not address. A good answer may also note that a single family can mark its seasons one way and its deaths another, which is what in fact happened. Partial: naming the two traditions with nothing said about neither having to be given up is approaching. (HSS-7.5)","A local tradition has no central organization to negotiate with, no landholdings to tax and no leadership to appoint, so a government mostly leaves it alone or patronizes particular shrines. A textual tradition has houses that own land and people, schools, hierarchies and sometimes armed followings, so a government must decide whether to fund it, tax it, appoint to it or restrain it. Property and organization are what make an institution a political matter. Partial: one of the two kinds of body described is approaching. (HSS-7.5)"]},{std:["HSS-7.7"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing Andean and Meso-American stone building done without iron tools, wheels or draught animals",html:()=>`<p class="big">Neither civilization had iron tools, wheeled vehicles or animals strong enough to pull a load. Both built in stone on a very large scale.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Inca walls at Cuzco and Sacsayhuaman</th><th>Maya and Mexica stepped pyramids</th></tr>
     <tr><td>The stone</td><td>very large blocks, each cut to fit its neighbors, no mortar</td><td>smaller cut blocks and rubble, faced with cut stone and plaster</td></tr>
     <tr><td>How it was shaped</td><td>pounded and ground with harder stone hammers, fitted by repeated trial</td><td>cut with stone tools, plastered and painted</td></tr>
     <tr><td>How it stood up</td><td>weight and the fit of the joints; the walls have survived earthquakes</td><td>a solid mass built up in stepped layers, often enlarged by building over the old one</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["Fitting each block to its neighbors by repeated trial is slow work. Name one advantage it gives in that particular place.","The pyramids were often enlarged by building a new one over the old. Explain one practical and one political reason to do it that way.","No iron, no wheels and no draught animals. Explain what the one remaining resource was, and what that means about the society that built these."],key:["Joints that fit tightly and carry weight directly hold together when the ground moves, because the blocks can shift slightly and settle back instead of cracking a mortar line. In an earthquake region that is worth the extra work, and the survival of the walls is the evidence. Partial: noting the tight joints with nothing said about earthquakes is approaching. (HSS-7.7)","Practical: the old structure is already a solid base, so building over it saves the work of a foundation and of clearing the site. Political: the new ruler’s building contains and covers his predecessor’s, which is a visible claim to inherit and surpass it, and the ceremonies attached to the place carry on without a break. Partial: one of the practical and the political reasons given is approaching. (HSS-7.7)","Human labor, organized in very large numbers over long periods. That means a society able to feed workers who are not farming, to call on labor as an obligation, and to direct, house and supervise it for years, which implies a state with authority, stores of food and record keeping. The buildings are evidence of government as much as of engineering. Partial: naming the labor with nothing said about what it implies about the state is approaching. (HSS-7.7)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Ledgers and Laws of Nature",short:"Ledgers",parts:[{std:["HSS-7.8"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Florentine banking, double entry bookkeeping and the patronage it paid for",html:()=>`<p class="big">Florentine banking firms of the 1300s and 1400s had branches in London, Bruges, Lyon, Rome and Naples. A merchant could pay money to a branch in one city and have his partner collect it in another, so cash never traveled the road. The firms kept their books by double entry: every transaction was written twice, once as a debit and once as a credit, so the two columns had to agree and an error showed up as a difference. The largest of these families came to dominate the city’s politics as well, and spent heavily on buildings, paintings and scholars.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("coin",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("palette",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain how paying in one city and collecting in another protects a merchant, and what the bank earns from it.","Writing every transaction twice is more work. Explain what it buys you.","A banking family came to run the city and spent heavily on art and buildings. Explain how spending on art serves a family in that position."],key:["Coin and bullion on the road can be stolen or lost at sea, and carrying it costs guards and time. A paper instrument carries no value to a thief who cannot present it, so the risk disappears. The bank earns a fee, and earns on the exchange rate between the two currencies, and it has the use of the money in the meantime. Partial: one of the merchant's gain and the bank's gain named is approaching. (HSS-7.8)","A check. If the two sides do not balance, there is a mistake or a theft somewhere and you know to look, which is what lets an owner in Florence trust accounts kept by a manager in London. It also makes it possible to see what the business is actually worth and what it owes, rather than only how much cash is in the drawer. Bookkeeping of that kind is what allows a firm to be large and spread out. Partial: describing the method with nothing said about trusting a distant manager is approaching. (HSS-7.8)","Buildings and paintings are public and permanent. They put the family’s name on the city, present it as the patron of the place rather than merely its richest household, and make the case that its wealth benefits everyone. For a family whose formal position was often no office at all, visible generosity was how power was held and justified. Partial: saying they wanted to look good with nothing said about holding power without an office is approaching. (HSS-7.8)"]},{std:["HSS-7.9"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Catholic Church's own reform program at Trent and why it was both answer and change",html:()=>`<p class="big">Between 1545 and 1563 Catholic bishops and theologians met in a long series of sessions at Trent. The Council restated the doctrines the Protestants had challenged. At the same time it ordered changes in practice. Bishops were to live in the dioceses they governed instead of drawing the income from elsewhere. A seminary was to be founded in every diocese to train priests properly. The Index of Prohibited Books was drawn up. The selling of offices and of indulgences was restricted. New orders were founded as well, above all the Jesuits, who ran schools and sent members as missionaries as far as India, Japan and the Americas.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("board",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["Bishops were ordered to live in the dioceses they governed. Explain what problem that rule was fixing.","The Council restated the old doctrine and reformed the practices at the same time. Explain why doing both at once was a deliberate strategy.","The Jesuits ran schools and traveled far overseas as missionaries. Name one effect of that on parts of the world outside Europe."],key:["Absentee bishops drew the income of a diocese and never went there, so the work was left to poorly paid and often poorly trained priests, and nobody with authority was answerable for what happened locally. It was one of the abuses the Protestants had pointed at, and it could be ended by an order without conceding any point of doctrine. Partial: naming the abuse with nothing said about why it could be ended without conceding doctrine is approaching. (HSS-7.9)","The Protestant criticisms were of two kinds: that the doctrine was wrong and that the Church behaved badly. Conceding the second removed most of the practical grievances and the easiest arguments, while conceding nothing on the first. Reforming the conduct was how the doctrine was defended, and it made the Catholic Church much harder to attack. Partial: naming the reforms with no distinction between conduct and doctrine is approaching. (HSS-7.9)","Any one: schools, printing and European learning were established in places far from Europe; local languages were studied, written down and dictionaries compiled, sometimes the first written record of them; information about those regions flowed back to Europe in long missionary reports historians still use; and the missions traveled with European trade and colonial power, so religious, commercial and political activity arrived together, which is why they were welcomed in some places and expelled from others, including Japan. Partial: naming a religious result rather than one outside religion is approaching, since that is what the question asks for. (HSS-7.9)"]},{std:["HSS-7.10"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the claim that nature is described in mathematics, and what that method requires",html:()=>`<p class="big">Investigators of the 1600s increasingly said that the way to describe how nature behaves is with numbers and geometry rather than with qualities such as heaviness or a tendency to seek its own place. A planet’s path was written as a curve with a rule; a falling body’s distance was written as a relation between distance and time squared; the pressure and the volume of a trapped gas were written as a product that stayed constant. Each of these had to be measured first. Each was published so that others could measure it again.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("calculator",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["A relation written in numbers can be tested in a way that a description in qualities cannot. Explain the difference.","Every one of these rules required measurement with an instrument. Name one reason progress in this period depended on instrument makers.","Name one kind of question about nature that this method is not able to answer, and explain why."],key:["A numerical relation says exactly what value you should get, so one careful measurement can contradict it. A description such as heavy things seek the ground does not tell you how fast or how far, so no measurement can ever disagree with it. Putting a number on a claim is what makes it refutable, which is why it can be built on. Partial: saying numbers are better with no account of being refutable is approaching. (HSS-7.10)","A rule can only be found and checked as accurately as the measuring allows, so better clocks, lenses, balances, thermometers, barometers and graduated scales were the limit on what could be discovered. Many of the period’s advances followed an improvement in an instrument, and the people who ground lenses and cut gear teeth were as necessary as the people who wrote the books. Partial: naming the instruments with nothing said about them setting the limit on what could be found is approaching. (HSS-7.10)","Any question that is not about measurable quantities: whether something is just, what a person ought to do, what a work of art means, why there is anything at all. The method works by measuring and comparing numbers, so a question with nothing to measure falls outside it. A good answer notices this is a limit on the method, not a fault in it. Partial: naming a question that could be measured is approaching, since the question asks what falls outside the method. (HSS-7.10)"]},{std:["HSS-7.11"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the cost of absolute monarchy and the political consequences of paying for it",html:()=>`<p class="big">Several European kings in the 1600s claimed to rule without needing the consent of an assembly. Holding that position cost money.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>What it cost</th><th>Why it was necessary to the claim</th></tr>
     <tr><td>a standing army loyal to the crown</td><td>a king who must ask nobles for their troops depends on them</td></tr>
     <tr><td>salaried officials across the country</td><td>officials who are paid by the crown answer to it, not to a local lord</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a great palace and a court</td><td>nobles kept at court for honors are nobles not building power at home</td></tr>
     <tr><td>wars</td><td>prestige, territory and trade routes, and an army is useless unused</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("key",74)}${ico("coin",74)}${ico("flag",74)}</div>`,qs:["Pick two rows and explain how each one took power away from the nobility.","All four cost money, and the king had claimed he did not need an assembly’s consent to raise it. Explain the difficulty that creates.","Many of these kings ended up selling offices, titles and monopolies, and borrowing heavily. Name one long term problem each of those creates.","England took a different path, with a parliament that controlled taxation. Name one advantage that arrangement gave an English government that an absolute monarch did not have."],key:["Any two handled correctly: a standing army means the king no longer needs the nobles’ private troops, so their main bargaining chip is gone; paid officials replace local lords as the government the ordinary person deals with; keeping nobles at court for honors turns them from regional powers into courtiers competing for the king’s notice; and war gives the crown a reason to command them rather than negotiate. Partial: one way named is approaching. (HSS-7.11)","Taxes are much harder to collect from people who have not agreed to them. Without an assembly to consent, the crown has no easy, legitimate way to raise large sums, so it must squeeze the groups least able to resist, invent new charges, sell things it owns or borrow. Refusing to ask makes the money dearer. Partial: saying he could tax as he liked is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-7.11)","Selling offices puts unsuitable people in posts they then treat as property, and the income is taken once while the post costs the state forever. Selling monopolies raises prices and blocks competitors, which slows the economy that is being taxed. Borrowing heavily means interest payments that grow until they crowd out everything else, and a default that ruins the crown’s credit when it next needs money. Partial: one of the three methods criticized is approaching. (HSS-7.11)","Credit and consent. A government whose taxes are voted by a parliament that includes the people who will pay them can borrow at much lower interest, because lenders believe the taxes to repay them will be voted. It can also raise more in total, since the taxpayers have agreed. Paradoxically the king who had to ask could spend more than the king who did not. Partial: naming the parliament with nothing said about cheaper borrowing is approaching. (HSS-7.11)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Life Under the Ice",short:"Under Ice",parts:[{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using sealed chamber readings at four depths to explain photosynthesis and respiration together",html:()=>`<p class="big">The expedition lowered sealed chambers of sea water holding ice algae and measured the carbon dioxide change in one hour.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Chamber</th><th>Light reaching it (percent of surface)</th><th>Carbon dioxide change in one hour</th><th>Oxygen change</th></tr>
     <tr><td>0.5 m under clear ice</td><td>42</td><td>down 310 ppm</td><td>up</td></tr>
     <tr><td>0.5 m under snow covered ice</td><td>4</td><td>down 35 ppm</td><td>barely up</td></tr>
     <tr><td>12 m down</td><td>1</td><td>up 20 ppm</td><td>down</td></tr>
     <tr><td>0.5 m under clear ice, algae filtered out</td><td>42</td><td>up 4 ppm</td><td>down slightly</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("snowflake",74)}${ico("fish",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["The top chamber took carbon dioxide out and the 12 m chamber put it in. Explain both, naming the process in each.","Compare the two chambers at 0.5 m. Name the only thing different between them, and say what it tells you.","The algae's new mass is mostly carbon. Name where that carbon came from, and say what the algae did with the light energy."],key:["In the top chamber the algae had enough light for photosynthesis, which takes carbon dioxide in and releases oxygen, and it ran faster than the respiration also going on, so the net reading fell by 310 ppm. At 12 m only 1 percent of the light arrived, so photosynthesis was too slow to keep up with respiration, which releases carbon dioxide and uses oxygen, so the net reading rose. Both processes run in the algae; which one shows depends on the light. Partial: accounting for the top chamber with nothing said about respiration outrunning photosynthesis at 12 m is approaching. (LS1)","One had snow on the ice above it and one did not, so the light reaching it was 4 percent instead of 42. The carbon dioxide taken in fell from 310 ppm to 35, which shows light was what limited photosynthesis, since depth, temperature, water and algae were the same. The fourth chamber is the control: with the algae filtered out nothing took carbon dioxide in at all. Partial: naming the snow as the only difference with nothing said about light being what limited photosynthesis is approaching. (LS1)","From the carbon dioxide dissolved in the sea water. The algae used light energy to rearrange the carbon, hydrogen and oxygen atoms from carbon dioxide and water into sugar and released the leftover oxygen, and the light energy ends up stored in the chemical bonds of that sugar rather than remaining as light. Partial: naming the dissolved carbon dioxide with nothing said about the light energy ending up in the bonds of the sugar is approaching. (LS1)"]},{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using hauling measurements to explain cellular respiration, mass loss and the uses of food molecules",html:()=>`<p class="big">A team member was measured in the hut and again after two hours hauling a sledge at minus 20 degrees.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Measure</th><th>In the hut</th><th>After two hours hauling</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Oxygen used each minute</td><td>0.3 L</td><td>2.4 L</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Carbon dioxide produced each minute</td><td>0.25 L</td><td>2.1 L</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Food energy used an hour</td><td>290 kJ</td><td>2,600 kJ</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Body mass</td><td>71.0 kg on day 1</td><td>69.4 kg on day 7</td></tr></table>
     <p>Rations on a polar journey are deliberately fat heavy.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sled",74)}${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("pack",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name the process using the oxygen and producing the carbon dioxide, and say what it does to the food molecules.","The team member lost 1.6 kg in a week while eating every day. Explain where that mass went, in terms of molecules.","Explain why fat rather than sugar is carried on a sledge, and name one job besides supplying energy that food molecules do."],key:["Cellular respiration. The cells break the bonds in sugars and fats and, using oxygen, rearrange the atoms into carbon dioxide and water, releasing the energy those bonds held. The food molecules are not destroyed; their atoms leave the body as the carbon dioxide breathed out and as water. Partial: naming cellular respiration with nothing said about the food molecules' atoms leaving as carbon dioxide and water is approaching. (LS1)","Their body broke down more stored fat and sugar than the food replaced, because hauling at minus 20 used more energy an hour than the rations supplied. Breaking those molecules down sends their atoms out as carbon dioxide in the breath and as water, so the mass left the body as gas and liquid. Mass is not simply burned away; it leaves as the products of respiration. Partial: saying the body used up its stored fat, with nothing said about the atoms leaving in the breath and as water, is approaching. (LS1)","Fat molecules store far more energy per gram than sugar, so a sledge can carry more days of energy for the same weight, which matters when everything is hauled. Besides energy, food molecules are taken apart and rearranged into the body's own material: muscle protein, new cell membranes, and replacements for skin and blood. Growth and repair are rearranged food atoms, which is why a diet needs more than fuel. Partial: naming fat's greater energy per gram with no second job named for food molecules is approaching. (LS1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"A Short Food Chain",short:"Ice Edge",parts:[{std:["LS2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a limiting condition through three populations and naming patterns of interaction",html:()=>`<p class="big">Counts taken along four stretches of the ice edge in the same week.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Stretch</th><th>Open water in the ice (percent)</th><th>Ice algae (mg per liter)</th><th>Krill (per cubic meter)</th><th>Seals counted</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>30</td><td>11.4</td><td>240</td><td>19</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>4</td><td>1.2</td><td>28</td><td>3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>28</td><td>10.8</td><td>225</td><td>18</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>29</td><td>11.1</td><td>230</td><td>2, a research camp sits here</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("seal",74)}${ico("whale",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}</div>`,qs:["Trace the effect of open water through three of these populations, naming the resource that limits each.","Stretch D has as much krill as A and C but almost no seals. Explain how that can be, and name the interaction between seal and krill.","Name a mutualism, a competition and a parasitism you would expect in this ecosystem, with an example of each."],key:["Open water lets light into the sea, so stretch B with 4 percent open water has only 1.2 mg of algae a liter against about 11 in the others; light limits the algae. Fewer algae means less food for krill, 28 a cubic meter against about 230; food limits the krill. Fewer krill means less food for seals, 3 against 18 or 19; food limits the seals. One physical condition reaches three levels. Partial: tracing the effect through two of the three populations, or through all three with the limiting resource named for only one, is approaching. (LS2)","The interaction is predation, with the seal as predator and the krill as prey. In stretch D the food is there, so food is not what is missing; the research camp is, and the noise and activity keep the seals away. A population is set by its resources and also by other factors including disturbance, so a place can have plenty of food and few animals. Partial: naming predation with nothing said about the camp's disturbance rather than food being what keeps the seals away is approaching. (LS2)","Mutualism: algae living inside the tissue of some sea animals, where the algae get shelter and the host gets sugar, and both gain. Competition: two seals working the same breathing hole, or krill and small fish feeding on the same algae. Parasitism: a worm living in a seal's gut, taking food from it without killing it. These same patterns of interaction turn up in every ecosystem, which is what makes them worth naming. Partial: two of the three interactions named with examples is approaching, and parasitism is usually the one left out. (LS2)"]},{std:["LS2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining energy loss along a chain, matter cycling, and solutions after a disruption",html:()=>`<p class="big">The expedition's energy figures for one square meter of the ice edge over a summer.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Who is in it</th><th>Energy stored in a summer (kJ per square meter)</th></tr>
     <tr><td>producers</td><td>ice algae and phytoplankton</td><td>6,100</td></tr>
     <tr><td>plant eaters</td><td>krill, copepods</td><td>580</td></tr>
     <tr><td>animal eaters</td><td>fish, seals, seabirds</td><td>53</td></tr>
     <tr><td>decomposers</td><td>bacteria in the water and on the sea floor</td><td>all the matter returns through here</td></tr></table>
     <p>In a warm year the sea ice broke up five weeks early and the algae had no underside of ice to grow on.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("penguin",74)}${ico("fish",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["The three figures fall to about a tenth each step. Account for the missing energy and say what it means for how many seals a stretch of ice edge can hold.","A carbon atom in an ice algae cell is in another algae cell two years later. Describe its path and say which of matter and energy cycles.","Predict two effects of the ice breaking up five weeks early, and name one thing people could do to protect the ice edge's variety of life, with the reason it would work."],key:["Most was released as thermal energy while the organisms moved, fed and kept their bodies going, and some stayed in parts never eaten, such as shells and waste, which pass to the decomposers. Only about a tenth is stored at the step above, so a square meter that supports 6,100 kJ of algae supports only 53 kJ of seals and birds. The number of seals is set by the energy the algae captured, not by how much room there is. Partial: naming the thermal energy with nothing said about the number of seals being set by the energy rather than by room is approaching. (LS2)","The algae is eaten or dies and sinks, bacteria break the remains down and release carbon dioxide into the water, and another algae cell takes that carbon dioxide in and photosynthesis builds it into sugar and then into cell material. Matter cycles and can be used again. The energy does not: it left as thermal energy during respiration, which is why the system needs the sun again every summer. Partial: tracing the carbon back into an algae cell with nothing said about matter cycling while energy does not is approaching. (LS2)","Effects, any two: the algae lose the underside of the ice they grow on, so much less energy enters the system that summer; the krill that hatch in spring find little food, so their numbers fall; the fish, seals and seabirds that depend on krill have a poor year and some move or fail to raise young; and the timing goes wrong, since animals that breed on a calendar arrive after the food has passed. A solution with a reason, any one: cut the emissions warming the ocean and the air, because the break up date is driven by that warming and nothing local changes it; set catch limits on krill, because a population under two pressures at once recovers more slowly; and protect areas where the ice edge forms reliably, because a species that survives somewhere can repopulate the rest. Partial: two effects predicted with a solution named but no reason given for why it works is approaching. (LS2)"]}]},{id:28,title:"What the Ice Writes Down",short:"Ice Core",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at both tables. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading an ice and rock core for its processes and comparing geoscience rates",html:()=>`<p class="big">A core was drilled through the ice cap and the rock beneath, and the station keeps rate records.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Depth</th><th>What it is</th><th>What is in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>0 to 80 m</td><td>layered ice</td><td>one light and one dark band a year, dust in the dark bands, air bubbles</td></tr>
     <tr><td>80 to 310 m</td><td>layered ice, the bands thinner</td><td>a gray ash band at 196 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>310 to 312 m</td><td>unsorted mud, sand and angular rock</td><td>rock pieces scratched on one face</td></tr>
     <tr><td>below 312 m</td><td>gray layered rock</td><td>shell fossils, flat thin beds</td></tr></table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Process</th><th>Rate measured at the station</th></tr>
     <tr><td>ice at the surface moving towards the sea</td><td>24 m a year</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the ice cap thickening from new snow</td><td>11 cm of ice a year</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a piece calving off the glacier front</td><td>in a few seconds</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the land recovering where the ice has gone</td><td>9 mm a year</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("shell",74)}${ico("compass",74)}</div>`,qs:["The rock below 312 m holds shell fossils in flat thin beds. Name the process that turned sediment into that rock, and contrast it with how the ice above was made.","The ice bands are thinner from 80 m down, and there is one light and one dark band a year. Explain what makes the bands thinner with depth, and say how a scientist uses them to date the ash at 196 m.","Using the rate table, name a process here that works in seconds. Name one that works in a year, and one that takes thousands of years. Then say why a scientist needs all three records."],key:["Burial, compaction and cementing: shells and mud settled in flat beds on a sea floor, more sediment pressed down and squeezed the water out, and minerals in that water cemented the grains into rock. The ice above records something different: snow fell, was buried by more snow and compressed into ice, which is the sun driven water cycle plus gravity rather than deposition on a sea floor. The same core holds a record from a sea and a record from the air. Partial: naming the burial, compaction and cementing with no contrast drawn against how the ice above was made is approaching. (MS-ESS2)","Deeper ice has carried the weight of all the ice above it for longer, so each year's layer is squeezed thinner than it was when it fell. A scientist counts the pairs of bands downwards, one pair to a year, the way tree rings are counted, and the number of pairs above 196 m gives the number of years since that ash fell. Thinning means the count, not the depth, gives the age. Partial: accounting for the thinning with nothing said about counting the band pairs rather than reading the depth is approaching. (MS-ESS2)","Seconds: a piece calving off the glacier front. A year: 24 m of surface movement, or 11 cm of new ice. Thousands of years: the land recovering at 9 mm a year, which is 9 m in a thousand years. All three are needed because they explain different things: the calving explains today's icebergs, the yearly rates explain where the ice will be in a lifetime, and the slow recovery explains the shape of the coast. A record that only covers one timescale makes the other two invisible. Partial: two of the three timescales matched to a process is approaching, and the thousand year recovery is usually the one left out. (MS-ESS2)"]},{std:["MS-ESS2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"using seafloor age, polar coal and trench data to explain plate motion",html:()=>`<p class="big">The station's geologist keeps three data sets pinned up.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Data set</th><th>What it shows</th></tr>
     <tr><td>ocean floor rock ages across a ridge south of the station</td><td>at the ridge, new; 90 km out, 2 million years; 450 km out, 10 million years; 990 km out, 22 million years. Matching on both sides</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what is in the rock under the ice cap</td><td>coal seams and fossils of large leafy trees and ferns, 250 million years old, in rock now 1,000 km from the nearest tree</td></tr>
     <tr><td>where ocean floor disappears</td><td>at trenches the ocean floor bends down beneath a neighboring plate; no ocean floor anywhere is older than about 200 million years</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("telescope",74)}${ico("polarBear",74)}</div>`,qs:["The ocean floor ages outwards at the same rate on both sides of the ridge. Say what is happening and what spreading rate the first pair of numbers gives.","Coal and fossils of large leafy trees lie in rock under a polar ice cap. Explain what that is evidence for.","The continents hold rock billions of years old, but no sea floor is older than about 200 million years. Explain, using the third row."],key:["New ocean floor is being made at the ridge and carried away from it in both directions, which is why rock is older the further it has gone. 90 km in 2 million years is 90,000 m in 2,000,000 years, which is 4.5 cm a year on each side, so the plates separate at about 9 cm a year. Partial: describing the sea floor spreading with the 4.5 cm a year not worked out from the 90 km and 2 million years is approaching. (MS-ESS2)","Large leafy trees and the swamp forests that make coal need warmth and long daylight, which this place does not have, so the rock cannot have formed here under these conditions. It is evidence that this land was once at a much lower latitude in a warm climate, and that the plate carrying it has moved thousands of kilometers since. The fossils did not move; the land did. Partial: saying the place used to be warm, with nothing said about the plate having carried the land from a lower latitude, is approaching. (MS-ESS2)","Sea floor is destroyed as well as made. Where it reaches a trench it bends down and sinks back into the mantle, so a piece of it lasts only from the ridge that made it to the trench that takes it, about 200 million years at most. Continental rock is less dense and does not sink that way, so it stays at the surface and can survive for billions of years. The sea floor is recycled; the continents are not. Partial: saying the sea floor is young, with nothing said about it sinking back into the mantle at the trench, is approaching. (MS-ESS2)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Supplies and Signals",short:"Supplies",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"classifying resources and explaining uneven distribution from the past processes that formed them",html:()=>`<p class="big">Nothing at the station comes from the ice. The supply list names where each thing comes from.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>What the station uses</th><th>Where it comes from</th><th>The past process that put it there</th></tr>
     <tr><td>fuel for the generator</td><td>an oil field 7,000 km away</td><td>sea life buried in mud and heated for over 100 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>fresh water</td><td>melted from the ice cap at the door</td><td>snow that fell here over the last few thousand years and was pressed into ice</td></tr>
     <tr><td>copper in the wiring</td><td>a mine in a mountain belt</td><td>hot water moving through cracked rock and depositing copper minerals as it cooled</td></tr>
     <tr><td>aluminum in the hut frame</td><td>bauxite from a tropical lowland</td><td>heavy tropical rain leaching everything but aluminum out of the rock over millions of years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>electricity from a wind turbine</td><td>the wind over the ridge</td><td>the sun heating the air unevenly, which makes the wind blow</td></tr></table>
     <p>The ice cap gains about 11 cm of ice a year from new snow.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wind",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("cabin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Sort these into renewable and nonrenewable and defend one choice with the process column.","Bauxite forms in tropical lowlands with heavy rain, and copper in cracked mountain rock with hot water moving through it. Explain why those two are never found in the same place, and what that means for a station at the pole.","Explain why a renewable resource can still be used up, and name one measurement that would tell the station whether it is melting too much ice."],key:["Renewable: the wind, because the sun keeps heating the air and the wind keeps blowing, and the melt water, because snow keeps falling, though slowly. Nonrenewable: the fuel, the copper and the aluminum. The fuel defends the point: it needed sea life buried and heated for over 100 million years, and nothing is replacing it at any rate anyone could use, so every liter burned is one fewer that exists. Partial: all five sorted correctly with no defense drawn from the process column is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Each needs a different set of conditions that cannot both be present at once. Bauxite needs warm heavy rain over millions of years on flat ground, which leaches away everything but the aluminum. Copper ore needs hot water circulating through broken rock, which happens where mountains are being built and heated. Those are different climates and different stages of a landscape's life. For the station it means almost everything has to be shipped in, because none of the past processes that make metals, fuel or soil ever happened on this ice. Partial: explaining why the two ores need different conditions with nothing said about what that means for a station on the ice is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Renewable means refilled, not unlimited. If the station melts more ice each year than the 11 cm of new snow adds, the store falls however renewable the source is. To tell, measure the depth melted for water each year against the snow accumulation measured at a stake, and watch the surface level at a fixed marker over several years: a level that keeps dropping is the sign that use is outrunning the refill. Partial: saying renewable does not mean unlimited with no measurement named that would show the station is melting too much is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]},{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"ordering hazard monitoring by warning time and matching preparations to the warning available",html:()=>`<p class="big">The station watches two hazards that decide when the sledge teams travel: a blizzard, and sea ice breaking up under a route. Nobody travels while a watch is on.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>What is watched</th><th>What it reports</th><th>Useful warning</th></tr>
     <tr><td>satellite images of cloud and wind</td><td>where the next storm is and which way it is moving</td><td>1 to 4 days</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a barometer and wind vane on the mast</td><td>pressure falling and wind rising</td><td>3 to 12 hours</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a drifting buoy on the sea ice</td><td>the ice moving and cracking</td><td>2 to 12 hours</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a thermometer and anemometer at the route marker</td><td>the wind speed and temperature on the route itself</td><td>minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>40 years of station logs with storm and break up dates</td><td>how often, in which weeks, over which routes</td><td>no timing</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("antenna",74)}${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}</div>`,qs:["Order the five from the most warning to the least, and name the one that gives no warning of a coming event.","The 40 years of logs cannot forecast a single blizzard. Name two decisions they can settle, and say what question they answer.","The route instruments give minutes. Name one measure that only works if it is prepared in advance and one that can be taken inside those minutes. Then explain what decides which you can rely on."],key:["Most to least: the satellite images, 1 to 4 days; the barometer and wind vane, 3 to 12 hours, and the drifting buoy, 2 to 12 hours; the route instruments, minutes. The 40 years of logs give no warning of a coming event, because they report how often and in which weeks these have happened, not when the next one will come. Partial: the four instruments ordered correctly with the 40 years of logs not identified as giving no warning of a coming event is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Any two: which weeks of the year to plan sledge journeys in; which routes to use and which to avoid, since the logs record where the ice has broken up; where to site a depot and how strongly to anchor it; and how many spare days of food a party should carry. They answer how often, when in the year, and where, not when next. Planning questions are answered by a long record; a departure on a given morning is answered by a forecast. Partial: two decisions named with nothing said about the logs answering how often and where rather than when next is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Prepared in advance: depots stocked along the route, a tent that can be put up in a wind, flagged markers every 100 m so a route can be followed in poor visibility, and a radio schedule. Taken inside minutes: stopping, putting the prepared tent up, and calling the station. What decides it is the warning time: with minutes you can only use what already exists, so the real work of reducing a short warning hazard is done months earlier, while a hazard with days of warning can still be answered by a decision made on the day. Partial: both kinds of measure named with nothing said about the warning time deciding which can be relied on is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"The Cold Laboratory",short:"Matter",parts:[{std:["MS-PS1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"classifying substances from atomic models, judging evidence for a reaction, and applying conservation of mass",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four substances were tested in the station laboratory.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Substance</th><th>What one particle is made of</th><th>Melting point</th><th>Behavior in water</th></tr>
     <tr><td>sea salt (sodium chloride)</td><td>sodium and chlorine atoms in a repeating grid</td><td>801 C</td><td>dissolves, and the solution carries a current</td></tr>
     <tr><td>nitrogen from the air cylinder</td><td>2 nitrogen atoms joined</td><td>-210 C</td><td>barely dissolves</td></tr>
     <tr><td>titanium from a tent pole</td><td>titanium atoms only</td><td>1,668 C</td><td>does not dissolve</td></tr>
     <tr><td>glycol in the antifreeze</td><td>2 carbon, 6 hydrogen and 2 oxygen atoms joined</td><td>-13 C</td><td>mixes completely, no current</td></tr></table>
     <p>A strip of magnesium from a flare was then put in dilute acid. Before: a shiny gray ribbon and a clear liquid. After: fizzing, no ribbon left, a clear liquid that dries to a white solid that dissolves in water, and a gas that burned with a pop. In an open beaker the beaker and contents went from 95.2 g to 95.0 g; in a stoppered flask they stayed at 95.2 g.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["Two of these are elements. Name them, explain how the particle column shows it, and say how many atoms are in one glycol particle.","Name two pieces of evidence that the magnesium and the acid reacted chemically, and say why a gas appearing is stronger evidence than a solid disappearing.","Explain both mass results and what they say about the atoms."],key:["Nitrogen and titanium. Nitrogen is two nitrogen atoms joined, one kind of atom only, so it is an element even though its particle has two atoms; titanium is titanium atoms only. A compound has two or more kinds of atom joined, which is what sea salt and glycol have. One glycol particle has 2 + 6 + 2 = 10 atoms. Partial: naming the titanium alone is approaching, since nitrogen's two joined atoms are the trap in this question, and so is naming both with the 10 atoms of glycol not counted. (MS-PS1)","Any two: a gas was produced where none existed before, and it burns, which neither the metal nor the acid did; the shiny gray ribbon is gone and cannot be recovered by drying; and a new white solid is left that dissolves in water, which the magnesium did not. New substances with new properties is the test. A gas appearing is stronger than a solid disappearing because a solid also disappears by dissolving, which makes nothing new and can be undone, while a burnable gas was not present in either starting substance and so had to be made. Partial: two pieces of evidence given with nothing said about a solid also disappearing by dissolving is approaching. (MS-PS1)","Both say the atoms were only rearranged. In the stoppered flask the hydrogen gas that formed could not leave, so every atom stayed inside and the mass held at 95.2 g. In the open beaker 0.2 g of that gas escaped into the room, so the beaker weighs less while the total mass of the beaker, its contents and the escaped gas is unchanged. Mass is conserved in a chemical reaction; an open container simply loses track of some of the products. Partial: saying the gas escaped from the open beaker, with nothing said about the total mass including the escaped gas being unchanged, is approaching. (MS-PS1)"]},{std:["MS-PS1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting a melting curve for sea ice, comparing natural and synthetic materials, and specifying a warming device",html:()=>`<p class="big">A block of sea ice was warmed in the laboratory at a steady energy input and read at the times shown.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Minute</th><td>0</td><td>5</td><td>10</td><td>40</td><td>80</td><td>90</td><td>100</td></tr>
     <tr><th>Temperature (C)</th><td>-24</td><td>-13</td><td>-2</td><td>-2</td><td>-2</td><td>12</td><td>25</td></tr></table>
     <p>The station's rope is a synthetic made from oil. Its mittens are wool from sheep.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mitten",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}${ico("sheep",74)}</div>`,qs:["The energy going in never changed, yet from minute 10 to minute 80 the reading held at minus 2. Describe what the particles were doing then and in the stretches either side.","Name the natural resource behind the rope and the mittens, the kind of change that makes each one, and which can be replaced.","A sample has to stay above minus 5 degrees for six hours in a box on a sledge, with no power. Describe a device that would release thermal energy into the box. Say what the change inside it must do with energy. Then name one measurement that would show whether it meets the need."],key:["Before minute 10 the arriving energy made the particles in the solid ice vibrate faster, which is what a rising temperature measures. From minute 10 to 80 it went into breaking the attractions holding the particles in the fixed ice arrangement, freeing them to slide past one another, so they did not speed up and the thermometer held still. That is melting, and it is by far the longest stretch because separating every particle takes much more energy than warming the solid did. It happens at minus 2 rather than 0 because the salt in sea ice lowers the melting point. After minute 80 it was liquid and the arriving energy again made the particles move faster. Partial: naming the melting with nothing said about the arriving energy breaking the attractions rather than speeding the particles up is approaching. (MS-PS1)","The rope comes from crude oil, a nonrenewable resource, and the oil's molecules are reacted to build long new molecules that were not in the oil, a chemical change, which is what makes it synthetic. The mittens come from a sheep's fleece, a renewable resource, and the wool is washed, spun and knitted, a physical change, since the fiber is the same substance at the end. A fleece grows back in a year; the oil took over 100 million years. Partial: both resources named with the physical and chemical change not told apart is approaching. (MS-PS1)","A sealed warmer where iron powder, salt and air react, or a sealed pouch of a salt that releases energy as it crystallizes. In each the change is exothermic: the products store less energy in their bonds, or in their arrangement, than the starting materials did, and the difference leaves as thermal energy, warming the box. A measurement, any one with a target: the lowest temperature in the box over the six hours, the number of hours it stays above minus 5, and the degrees of warming per gram of warmer, which says how many are needed. Partial: naming a warmer with no measurement given, or with the change not identified as one that releases energy, is approaching. (MS-PS1)"]}]},{id:31,title:"The Sledge Box",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria and constraints and comparing competing designs",html:()=>`<p class="big">Samples have to ride on a sledge for six hours at minus 30 and stay above minus 5. The station has plywood, closed cell foam, sheepskin, reflective film, chemical warmers, and a 9 kg limit for the whole box.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Hours above minus 5</th><th>Box mass (kg)</th><th>Warmers used</th><th>Build time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: plywood box, no lining</td><td>1.5</td><td>4.2</td><td>0</td><td>40 min</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: plywood with 3 cm foam</td><td>5.0</td><td>5.6</td><td>0</td><td>70 min</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: plywood with 3 cm foam and 2 warmers</td><td>9.5</td><td>6.4</td><td>2</td><td>75 min</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D: plywood with 8 cm foam and 2 warmers</td><td>14.0</td><td>9.8</td><td>2</td><td>110 min</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("sled",74)}${ico("compass",74)}</div>`,qs:["Write the problem in one sentence naming what must be achieved, with no material or device in it.","Write two criteria and two constraints, and label them.","Using the table, say which designs meet the criteria, which fails a constraint, and which you would pick, naming the trade off."],key:["Something like: keep samples above minus 5 degrees for six hours on a sledge in minus 30 air, in a package no heavier than 9 kg. It names the need, the temperature, the duration, the outside conditions and the weight limit, and no plywood, foam or warmer. Partial: a sentence that names foam or a warmer rather than the result to be achieved is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Criteria: the box holds the samples above minus 5 for at least six hours, and the longer it holds, the better. Constraints: a total mass of 9 kg or less, outside air at minus 30, a six hour journey, and only the plywood, foam, sheepskin, film and warmers on hand. A criterion measures how good the result is; a constraint is a limit the design may not break. Partial: two criteria and one constraint, or one of each, is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","B, C and D all last six hours or more, so they meet the temperature criterion; A at 1.5 hours does not. D fails the mass constraint at 9.8 kg against a 9 kg limit, so its 14 hours cannot be used. Pick C: 9.5 hours at 6.4 kg, with over three hours of margin and 2.6 kg to spare. The trade off is that C spends two warmers and 75 minutes to buy that margin, where B is lighter and simpler but has no margin at all if the journey runs long or the air is colder than minus 30. Partial: picking C with nothing said about D failing the 9 kg limit, or with no trade off named against B, is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]},{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing test data to improve a design and explaining iterative testing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Design C was tested four times.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Test</th><th>What changed</th><th>Hours above minus 5</th><th>Box mass (kg)</th><th>Note from the run</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>design C as built</td><td>9.5</td><td>6.4</td><td>the lid corner was cold to the touch</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>foam added at the lid corner</td><td>11.0</td><td>6.6</td><td>lid harder to close</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>foam at the lid corner and reflective film inside</td><td>11.5</td><td>6.7</td><td>film creased and tore in two places</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>foam at the lid corner, film, and 1 warmer instead of 2</td><td>7.0</td><td>6.2</td><td>still above the six hour need</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("pack",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which single change gave the biggest gain for its cost, and how do the numbers show it?","Test 4 changed one thing from test 3. Say what it tells you, and which design you would carry on the next journey and why.","Test 3's note says the film creased and tore. Say what that means for the design, and why reaching 11.5 hours is not the end of the work."],key:["Test 2, the foam at the lid corner. Hours above minus 5 went from 9.5 to 11.0, a gain of 1.5 hours, for 0.2 kg. Test 3's reflective film added only 0.5 hours for a further 0.1 kg and tore, so the lid corner foam did far more per gram. Test 1's note had already pointed at the lid corner, which is why that was the change worth making. Partial: naming the lid corner foam with the 1.5 hours gained for 0.2 kg not set against the film's half hour is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","It tells you the second warmer is worth about 4.5 hours, since dropping it took the box from 11.5 to 7.0 hours, and that one warmer still clears the six hour requirement with an hour to spare at 6.2 kg. Which to carry depends on what matters: for a six hour trip with margin, test 4 is lighter and uses half the warmers; for a journey that could be delayed, test 3's 11.5 hours is the safer choice. Because only one thing changed between the two tests, the cost of each option is known, which is what makes the decision possible. Partial: putting the second warmer's worth at about 4.5 hours with no design chosen and no reason given is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","It means the film, as fitted, does not survive the way the box is used, so its half hour of benefit cannot be relied on; it has to be mounted so the lid does not crease it, or dropped. Reaching 11.5 hours is not the end because every test here was one run, and the failure that matters showed up as wear rather than as a temperature. The design has to be run many times over a real route with what wears out written down, and the changes that follow tested again. Design is a cycle, not one winning run. Partial: saying the film has to be remounted or dropped with nothing said about why 11.5 hours is not the end of the work is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: One Idea, Many Parts",std:["RI.8.2"],needs:["RI.7.2"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use the article.",html:()=>`${floeBlock()}<ol class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px"><li>One central idea of the whole article is that the thing that makes the camp dangerous is also the reason the camp is there. How do the supporting ideas in the second paragraph, such as tents on skis, drums one person can roll, and the crack watch, relate to that central idea?</li><li>How does the last paragraph connect back to the first paragraph?</li></ol>`,key:["Each of those details is a way of living on a floor that moves and breaks, and that moving, breaking floor is exactly what the crew came to measure. So the supporting ideas about packing light and watching for cracks show how tightly the risk and the work are tied together, and they set up the last paragraph, where a floe that cracks under a tent has just handed the team a new number. (RI.8.2, central idea and its relationship to supporting ideas)","The first paragraph says nothing holds a floe in place and the camp travels with it, which reads like a warning. The last paragraph calls that same moving floor “the laboratory” and says the crew spends its days measuring the one thing that makes the camp dangerous. The fact introduced as a hazard at the start becomes the reason for the camp at the end. (RI.8.2, development of the central idea over the text)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Which Team Is Faster?",std:["8.EE.B.5"],needs:["7.RP.A.2"],n:2,dir:"Look at the graph. Answer out loud. Use your paper if you need it.",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Team Alfa's travel is shown by the graph. Team Charlie's travel fits the equation d = 11t, where d is miles and t is hours.</p><div class="figure">${teamAGraph()}</div><ol class="qlist"><li>What is the slope of Team Alfa's line? What does that slope mean for the team?</li><li>Which team travels faster, Alfa or Charlie? How do you know? If both leave camp at the same time on the same route, how far apart are they after 5 hours?</li></ol>`,key:["The slope is 8. Rise ÷ run = 24 ÷ 3 = 8, or 48 ÷ 6 = 8. Team Alfa travels 8 miles each hour, so the slope is the unit rate. (8.EE.B.5, slope as a unit rate)","Team Charlie. Its rate is the 11 in d = 11t, which is 11 miles an hour, and 11 is more than Alfa's 8. After 5 hours Alfa has gone 8 × 5 = 40 miles and Charlie has gone 11 × 5 = 55 miles, so they are 15 miles apart. This compares a graph with an equation. (8.EE.B.5, comparing two proportional relationships in different forms)"]},3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Mood and Voice",std:["L.8.1"],needs:["L.7.1"],n:3,dir:"Answer out loud. Say each fixed sentence the correct way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>One verb is in the wrong mood for a wish about something that is not true. Fix it: “If the wind was calmer, we would fly the survey today.”</li><li>This sentence shifts from active voice to passive voice. Make both parts active: “The crew raised the tent, and the stove was lit by the cook.”</li><li>“To reach the depot before dark, the team left at five.” Which words are the verbal phrase, what kind of verbal is it, and what job does it do?</li></ol>`,key:["If the wind were calmer, we would fly the survey today. The wind is not calmer, so the verb takes the subjunctive form were. (L.8.1, subjunctive mood)","The crew raised the tent, and the cook lit the stove. (L.8.1, fixing a shift from active to passive voice)","“To reach the depot before dark.” It is an infinitive phrase, because it starts with to plus the verb reach. It tells why the team left at five, so it works like an adverb. (L.8.1, verbals)"]},4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Both Sides",std:["8.EE.C.7"],needs:["7.EE.B.4"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>One outfitter rents a sled for $9 a day plus a $40 deposit. A second outfitter rents the same sled for $13 a day with no deposit. Write and solve an equation to find the number of days, d, at which the two cost the same.</li><li>Solve: 5(x + 2) = 3x + 18</li><li>How many solutions does this equation have? How do you know? 2(4x − 3) = 8x − 6</li></ol>`,key:["10 days. 9d + 40 = 13d. Subtract 9d: 40 = 4d. Divide by 4: d = 10. Check: 9 × 10 + 40 = 130 and 13 × 10 = 130. (8.EE.C.7)","x = 4. Distribute: 5x + 10 = 3x + 18. Subtract 3x: 2x + 10 = 18. Subtract 10: 2x = 8. Divide by 2: x = 4. Check: 5 × 6 = 30 and 3 × 4 + 18 = 30. (8.EE.C.7)","Infinitely many solutions. Distributing the left side gives 8x − 6 = 8x − 6, the same expression on both sides, so every value of x works. (8.EE.C.7)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Spot What Does Not Belong",std:["RI.8.8"],needs:["RI.7.8"],n:2,dir:"Read the memo to yourself. Then answer out loud.",html:()=>`${oneText(PHONE_TITLE,PHONE_SRC,PHONE)}<ol class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px"><li>What does Dr. Nakai want the board to do? What reason does he give, and what evidence backs it up?</li><li>One sentence in the memo is irrelevant evidence. Which one? Why does it not belong in this argument?</li></ol>`,key:["Claim: base camp should carry a second satellite phone this season. Reason: with only one phone, a single failure leaves camp with no way to call out. Evidence: in the February storm the antenna iced over and the radio log shows 31 hours out of contact with the supply plane while a crew of four was out on the floe; the log lists nine days last season when the single phone was offline for more than two hours; the $2,400 cost is less than one hour of charter flying time. (RI.8.8, delineate the argument)","The sentence that a survey at the university found most students think polar research is interesting. What students find interesting says nothing about whether one phone is enough or whether a second one is worth $2,400, so it does nothing to support the claim. (RI.8.8, recognizing irrelevant evidence)"]}};const READ_STD=["RI.7.2","RI.7.8","RI.7.9","RL.7.2","RL.7.6","RL.7.9","L.7.4","RI.8.2","RI.8.8"];const MATH_STD=["7.RP.A.2","7.RP.A.3","7.NS.A.3","7.EE.B.3","7.EE.B.4","7.G.A.2","7.G.B.4","7.G.B.6","7.SP.B.4","7.SP.C.8","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.7"];const G_FLOE=[["sea ice","the frozen crust that forms on top of the ocean in winter"],["floe","a wide slab of floating sea ice"],["drift camp","a research camp built on a floe, which moves as the floe moves"],["satellite","a machine in orbit above the earth that takes pictures and measurements from above"]];const GLOSS={"RI.7.2":G_FLOE,"RI.8.2":G_FLOE,"RI.7.8":[["snow machine","a snowmobile, a small engine-powered sled a driver rides"],["dogsled","a sled pulled by a team of dogs"],["survey","a trip to measure and map an area"],["plateau","a wide, high, flat stretch of land"]],"RI.8.8":[["satellite phone","a phone that calls through a satellite, used where there are no phone towers"],["antenna","the part of a radio or phone that sends and receives the signal"],["charter plane","a plane hired for one trip instead of a scheduled flight"]],"W.7.1":[["floe","a wide slab of floating sea ice"],["drilling site","a spot on the ice where the crew drills down to measure the ice and the water below"],["gravel strip","a flat line of gravel smooth enough for a small plane to land on"]],"W.7.2":[["floe ice","ice from a floating slab of sea ice; old floe ice looks blue and is nearly fresh"],["grit","tiny bits of sand or dirt"],["dog line","the rope a dog team is tied along at camp"]],"RL.7.2":[["thickness gauge","an instrument that reads how far down ice goes"],["log","the record book a station writes its readings in"],["porous","full of small holes, like a sponge"]],"RL.7.6":[["observer","a crew member whose job is to watch and record conditions"],["tripod","a three-legged stand for an instrument"]],"RL.7.9":[["auger","a hand drill with a spiral blade, used to cut a hole in ice"],["sledge runner","the long strip a sledge slides on"],["drift station","a research station built on moving sea ice"]],"RI.7.9":[["autonomous","working on its own, with nobody operating it"],["buoy","a float that carries instruments and is left in place"],["Arctic basin","the deep ocean around the North Pole"],["ton","a metric ton, 1,000 kilograms"]],"L.7.4":[["context","the words and sentences around a word, which often show what it means"],["word part","a prefix, root, or suffix that carries meaning of its own"]],"W.7.8":[["credible","able to be trusted"],["plagiarism","using someone else's words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to put it in your own words"],["citation","the list of who wrote something, what it is called, where it appeared, and when"]],"SL.7.4":[["salient","standing out as the most important"],["claim","the position you are arguing for"]]};const glossHtml=st=>dl(st.std.filter(x=>GLOSS[x]).reduce((a,x)=>a.concat(GLOSS[x].filter(g=>!a.includes(g))),[]));const FRAMES={"RI.7.2":["One central idea is ____.","Another central idea is ____.","The author builds this idea with the detail ____.","The article turns to the second idea when ____.","In short, the article explains ____."],"RI.8.2":["The central idea is ____.","The detail about ____ supports that idea because ____.","The last paragraph connects back to the first by ____."],"RI.7.8":["Dr. Achebe claims that ____.","One reason she gives is ____.","Her strongest evidence is ____, because ____.","This reason is weak because ____."],"RI.8.8":["Dr. Nakai wants the board to ____.","His reason is ____, and the evidence is ____.","The sentence about ____ does not belong because ____."],"W.7.1":["Base camp should be on ____.","One reason is ____. The fact box says ____.","Someone might say ____, but ____.","For that reason, ____."],"W.7.2":["A polar camp gets its water from ____.","First, the crew ____.","After that, ____.","As a result, ____.","That is how ____."],"RL.7.2":["A theme of the story is ____.","The idea grows when ____, and again when ____.","In short, the story is about ____."],"RL.7.6":["Ines sees the ice as ____, and Pavel sees it as ____.","The author builds the contrast by ____.","The story is told through ____, which means the reader ____."],"RL.7.9":["The history says observers ____. In the story, ____.","The history states ____, and the story shows it by ____.","The fiction adds ____, which the history does not give."],"RI.7.9":["Both texts take up the question of ____.","The article emphasizes ____, while Farrell emphasizes ____.","They agree that ____, but the article reads it as ____ and Farrell reads it as ____."],"L.7.4":["____ here means ____.","I worked it out from ____.","The other meaning of ____ is ____, and it does not fit because ____."],"W.7.3":["That morning, ____.","First I ____. Then ____.","“____,” said ____.","By the time ____, I ____."],"W.7.8":["I would use Source ____ because ____.","Note ____ is plagiarism because ____.","Farrell says ____ (Farrell, 2026).","Author: ____. Title: ____. Published in: ____. Year: ____."],"SL.7.4":["My claim is that the board should ____.","The strongest point is ____.","The evidence for it is ____.","So the board should ____."]};const framesHtml=st=>`<ul class="starters">${st.std.filter(x=>FRAMES[x]).reduce((a,x)=>a.concat(FRAMES[x]),[]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const fm=t=>`<p class="formula">${t}</p>`;const FORM={"7.RP.A.2":["Proportional relationships",fm("y = kx")+ul(["k is the constant of proportionality, or unit rate. k = y ÷ x.","In a table, y ÷ x is the same number in every column.","On a graph, it is a straight line through (0, 0). The point (1, k) shows the unit rate.","A constant rate of change is not always a proportional relationship. Check (0, 0)."])],"7.RP.A.3":["Percents",fm("part = percent × whole")+ul(["Change the percent to a decimal first: 35% = 0.35.","Sale price = price − discount, or price × (1 − percent off).","Percent change = amount of change ÷ original amount.","Percent error = how far off ÷ measured amount.","Simple interest: I = principal × rate × time in years."])],"7.NS.A.3":["Negative numbers",ul(["Adding a negative is the same as subtracting: −8 + (−10) = −8 − 10.","Negative × positive = negative. Negative ÷ positive = negative.","Below zero, below the surface, and money owed are negative.","Mean = sum ÷ how many numbers."])],"7.EE.B.3":["Multi-step problems",ul(["A fraction, a decimal, and a percent can all name the same amount. Pick the easiest form.","Estimate first with round numbers, then compare your exact answer to the estimate.","Two percent changes in a row cannot be added. Apply them one at a time."])],"7.EE.B.4":["Equations and inequalities",ul(["Name the unknown with a letter first, then write the equation from the words.","px + q = r: undo the + q first, then divide by p.","p(x + q) = r: divide by p first, then undo the + q. Or distribute first.","≤ means at most. ≥ means at least.","Solve an inequality like an equation, then say what the answer means."])],"8.EE.B.5":["Slope",ul(["Slope = rise ÷ run between two points on the line.","For a proportional relationship the slope is the unit rate.","In y = kx, the number k is the slope."])],"8.EE.C.7":["Variables on both sides",ul(["Get the variable terms on one side and the numbers on the other.","If you end with a false statement, such as 5 = −2, there is no solution.","If you end with a true statement, such as 6 = 6, every number is a solution."])],"7.G.A.2":["Drawing triangles",ul(["Three sides make a triangle only when the two shorter ones add to more than the longest one.","Three given side lengths give exactly one triangle.","Three given angles that add to 180 give infinitely many triangles, all the same shape in different sizes.","Angles in any triangle add to 180 degrees."])],"7.G.B.4":["Circles",fm("C = pi × d = 2 × pi × r")+fm("A = pi × r × r")+ul(["The radius is half the diameter.","Use 22/7 or 3.14 for pi unless you are told otherwise.","Area comes out in square units, circumference in plain units."])],"7.G.B.6":["Area, surface area, and volume",ul(["Area of a rectangle = length × width. Area of a triangle = 1/2 × base × height.","Break a composite figure into shapes you know, find each area, then add.","Volume of a right prism = area of the base × height.","Surface area = the total area of every face. In a box, each of the three different faces appears twice."])],"7.SP.B.4":["Comparing two samples",ul(["Measures of center: mean and median. Measures of variability: range, interquartile range, mean absolute deviation.","Mean absolute deviation = the average distance of the values from the mean.","Compare the gap between the two centers with the size of one mean absolute deviation. A gap much smaller than one is a small difference.","A random sample is what lets a few values stand in for the whole group."])],"7.SP.C.8":["Compound events",ul(["The probability of a compound event is the number of favorable outcomes out of all the outcomes.","For two things that do not affect each other, multiply the probabilities along a branch of the tree.","P(at least one) = 1 − P(none).","A simulation uses a coin, a spinner, or cards with the same probabilities, repeated many times."])]};const formHtml=st=>st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`).join("");let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(src){const s=src.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-");let i=0;if(!s)return"";const num=()=>{const a=i;while(i<s.length&&/[0-9.]/.test(s[i]))i++;const v=a===i?NaN:Number(s.slice(a,i));if(!isFinite(v))throw new Error("n");return v};const fac=()=>{if(s[i]==="-"){i++;return-fac()}if(s[i]==="("){i++;const v=expr();if(s[i]===")")i++;return v}return num()};const term=()=>{let v=fac();for(;;){if(s[i]==="*"){i++;v*=fac()}else if(s[i]==="/"){i++;v/=fac()}else if(s[i]==="(")v*=fac();else return v}};function expr(){let v=term();for(;;){if(s[i]==="+"){i++;v+=term()}else if(s[i]==="-"){i++;v-=term()}else return v}}try{const v=expr();if(i<s.length||!isFinite(v))return"Error";return String(+v.toPrecision(10))}catch(e){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","C","+","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">This calculator adds, subtracts, multiplies, and divides. For a negative number, press − before the number. Type a percent as a decimal: 35% is 0.35.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.slice(0,-1);calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval(calcE)}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷]$/.test(k))calcE=calcAns.replace("-","−");else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}const multBind=(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()});let nlMark=null;function nlineHtml(){return`<p class="small">Click the line to mark a number. The line runs from −20 to 20 by ones.</p>`+grid({alt:"Number line from negative 20 to 20. Click to mark a number.",cls:"nline",x0:-20,x1:20,y0:0,y1:1,sx:22,sy:30,gx:1,lx:5,gy:1,ly:1,fs:12,pr:7,pts:nlMark===null?[]:[[nlMark,.5]]})+`<p class="small">Marked: ${nlMark===null?"nothing yet":nlMark}</p>`}function nlineBind(b,r){const el=b.querySelector("svg.nline");if(el)el.onclick=e=>{const q=el.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*(880+34+18)/q.width,x=Math.round((vx-34)/22)-20;if(x<-20||x>20)return;nlMark=x;r()}}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;function pgridHtml(){return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div>`+grid({alt:"Blank coordinate grid from 0 to 20. Click to plot points.",cls:"pgrid",x0:0,x1:20,y0:0,y1:20,sx:20,sy:20,lx:2,ly:2,fs:11,pr:5,pts:gridPts,paths:gridJoin&&gridPts.length>1?[{pts:gridPts,c:"var(--coral)"}]:[]})+`<p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const el=b.querySelector("svg.pgrid");if(el)el.onclick=e=>{const q=el.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*452/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*444/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-34)/20),y=20-Math.round((vy-16)/20);if(x<0||x>20||y<0||y>20)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={gloss:{name:"Word help (glossary)",std:Object.keys(GLOSS),html:glossHtml},frames:{name:"Sentence frames",std:Object.keys(FRAMES),html:framesHtml},form:{name:"Formula sheet",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:multBind},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},nline:{name:"Number line with negatives",std:["7.NS.A.3","7.EE.B.4","7.SP.B.4"],html:nlineHtml,bind:nlineBind},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:["7.RP.A.2","8.EE.B.5"],html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"passage",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",std:["W.7.1","W.7.2"],msg:"If you read the fact box aloud, note it. The writing checks still stand."},{t:"gloss",msg:"Word help explains background words such as floe and snow machine. It does not give answers, but note that it was used."},{t:"frames",std:["RI.7.2","RI.7.8","RI.8.2","RI.8.8"],msg:"Sentence frames give the shape of the answer. Rate the evidence and thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["W.7.1"],msg:"The sentence frames supply the claim opener and the answer to the other side, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"frames",std:["W.7.2"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the linking words and the ending, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"form",std:["7.RP.A.2"],msg:"The formula sheet states the test for a proportional relationship, including the check at (0, 0), so the heater question shows applying a stated rule."},{t:"form",std:["7.RP.A.3"],msg:"The formula sheet gives the percent change, percent error, and simple interest formulas, so this stop shows using them, not recalling them."},{t:"form",std:["7.NS.A.3","7.EE.B.3","7.EE.B.4","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.7"],msg:"The formula sheet gives the rules and steps for this stop, so it shows applying them with a reference, not recall."},{t:"calc",std:["7.NS.A.3","7.EE.B.3"],msg:"7.NS.A.3 and 7.EE.B.3 include computing with negative numbers, fractions, and decimals. With the calculator, these parts show setting up the problem, not the computing."},{t:"calc",std:["7.RP.A.2","7.RP.A.3","7.EE.B.4","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.7"],msg:"The calculator did the arithmetic. Rate whether the student chose the right steps and explained them."},{t:"nline",std:["7.NS.A.3"],msg:"With the number line, the temperature questions show adding and subtracting with a visual model."},{t:"mult",std:MATH_STD,msg:"The multiplication chart supplied the facts. Rate the setup and the reasoning."},{t:"grid",std:["7.RP.A.2","8.EE.B.5"],msg:"If you plotted points for the student, note it. The student should still explain what the graph shows."},{t:"frames",std:["RL.7.2","RL.7.6","RL.7.9","RI.7.9","L.7.4"],msg:"Sentence frames give the shape of the answer. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["W.7.3","W.7.8","SL.7.4"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"gloss",std:["L.7.4"],msg:"Word help defines context and word part, and it glosses polar words. It does not give the three answers, but if the student read the glossary entry for a word that is also an item, do not rate that item."},{t:"form",std:["7.G.A.2","7.G.B.4","7.G.B.6","7.SP.B.4","7.SP.C.8"],msg:"The formula sheet gives the triangle conditions, the circle formulas, the area and volume rules, and the probability rules, so these stops show applying them with a reference, not recall."},{t:"calc",std:["7.G.B.4","7.G.B.6","7.SP.B.4","7.SP.C.8"],msg:"The calculator did the arithmetic. Rate whether the student chose the right formula or method and explained it."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:READ_STD.concat(["W.7.1","W.7.2"])}}});
